# The annotations of the game image: the single source of truth for src/game/*.asm (kit/mkgame.py).
# The format is in kit/mkgame.py, class Annotations. One line an entry:
#
#   @module NAME ; title            the entries from here on go to NAME.asm, the title heads the file
#   @about a paragraph              the module file's header text, one paragraph a line
#   $ADDR c Name ; title            a routine
#   > a description line
#   $ADDR b Name ; title            data (w words, t text, s space; "b Name 8" rows of 8)
#   @res graphics/NAME.bin          the entry is a resource: src/res/..., INCBIN
#   $ADDR Name ; comment            a label and comment inside an entry: on an instruction, or on the data line
#                                   that starts at ADDR (a data entry's rows are cut there)
#   @isub $ADDR LD HL,-6            the instruction written so
#   @equ $ADDR Name [N] ; comment    a variable past the image: an EQU, its operands by name
#   @inline $ADDR                   a routine that reads its data after the CALL
# The ROM, the system variables, the screen and the RAM past the loaded image ($E1A8-$FFFF)
# The data regions past the code, entries for the operands (step 3 shapes them)

@equ $028E KEY_SCAN ; the ROM's KEY-SCAN: DE = the key values ($FF none), NZ for more than two keys
@equ $031E K_TEST ; the ROM's K-TEST: E = KEY-SCAN's value, A = the character ($FF none)
@equ $03B5 BEEPER ; the ROM's BEEPER: HL = the period, DE = the cycles less one (it plays DE + 1 cycles, 2 DE + 2 speaker writes)
@equ $16B0 SET_MIN ; the ROM's SET-MIN: clears the editing area and the calculator stack
@equ $2D28 STACK_A ; the ROM's STACK-A: A onto the calculator stack
@equ $2D2B STACK_BC ; the ROM's STACK-BC: BC onto the calculator stack
@equ $2DA2 FP_TO_BC ; the ROM's FP-TO-BC: the last value off the calculator stack into BC (rounded)
@equ $3D00 CHAR_SET ; the ROM's character set, glyphs $20-$7F, 8 bytes each (upper case, digits, punctuation for the print routine)
@equ $4000 Screen 6144 ; the display file
@equ $5800 Attributes 768 ; the attributes
@equ $5C3A ERR_NR ; the ROM's IY base (ERR_NR): IY must hold it while the ROM runs
@equ $5C48 BORDCR ; the ROM's border colour, bits 3-5
@equ $FCFC Im2Jump 3 ; the IM 2 target: EI; JP Im2Handler (written by the entry); $FCFD patched to RET by the frame wait
@equ $FE00 Im2Table ; the IM 2 vector table, all $FC (I = $FE)

@alias StackTop StackSpace+$40 ; the top of the machine stack, $D000 (the first byte above it is BallSprites)
@isub $8000 LD SP,StackTop
@isub $80B3 LD SP,StackTop
@isub $8216 LD BC,$FEFE
@isub $801D LD DE,Im2Table+1
@isub $8750 LD DE,$B000
@isub $8F1F LD DE,$82FF
@isub $8F79 LD DE,$82FF
@isub $9059 LD DE,$85FF
@isub $90B3 LD DE,$85FF
@isub $9282 LD DE,$B700
@isub $928E LD DE,$B60F
@isub $92A8 LD DE,$B68A
@isub $94C5 LD HL,$4010
@isub $9684 LD HL,$1400
@isub $9BF1 LD HL,ShotsPerFrame-1
@isub $9D8C LD DE,CHAR_SET+704
@isub $B90F LD BC,500
@isub $B993 LD HL,-240
@isub $B9C2 LD BC,-20
@isub $B9EA LD BC,-20
@isub $BD18 LD DE,$8010
@isub $BDE9 LD BC,300
@isub $BE25 LD BC,900

# Fragment 01 (vp2): $8000-$86C0 (the entry, the sound loop, the interrupt, the input, the shot and aim tasks) and
# $9155-$9242 (the task scheduler). Counts: python3 tools/l1sum.py ADDR (builds game, w1, f1, p2, v1, m1).

# Video Pool stage 2, fragment 02 (physics): $86C1-$9154 and the physics tables $DBFA-$DEF3.
# Witnesses: tools/l1sum.py over game (c1 from the entry), w1, f1, p2, v1, m1; details in 02-physics-notes.md.

# Fragment 03 (annotator 03): $9243-$A161, drawing, HUD, scoring, turns, printing, racking.
# Coordinates: in DE / HL pairs the high byte is the pixel row y (0-191), the low byte the pixel column x (0-255),
# unless a comment says "row, column" (character cells).

# Annotator 04: $A162-$B8C4, the rack-in scripts of sets 1-5, the hall of fame and name entry, the menu.

# Annotator 05: the presents page and attract sequence $B8C5-$C041, the table editor $C042-$C5FF, the pen script
# $DA80-$DBF9 (the header's "logo data") and the development leftover $E014-$E1A7. Notes: 05-editor-notes.md.

# vp2: the data regions past the code that no other fragment owns: the free space and the stack, the ball sprites, the font.

@module main ; The entry, the restart, the foreground sound loop, task lists, the frame wait, the interrupt handler
@about The game is entered at GameStart $8000 (the turbo loader's JP). After the presents page it builds the IM 2 table and the jump at $FCFC, runs the menu, sets up a game and the aim, and falls into the foreground loop SoundLoop: from then on all the work of play runs in the interrupt as tasks (scheduler $9155); the foreground only plays the beeper sound a task requests in SoundRequest $827D and watches for BREAK in InputState $827C.
@about BREAK (CAPS SHIFT + SPACE, InputState bit 5) goes to BreakRestart: the tasks are cleared, the stack reset and the game restarts at the menu (GameRestart). The game never returns to BASIC.
$8000 c GameStart ; The game's entry: the stack, the ball records, the presents page
> Entered once by the turbo loader's JP $8000 (every build: game frame 5556, each play recording at its start). LD SP,$D000 (the stack below BallSprites), $9D2C resets the ball records, EI (still IM 1, the ROM's handler), $BD0B = 0, the presents page and attract sequence $B8C5 (returns on a key), $BD0B = $FF (so a later attract run is shorter), then falls into GameRestart.
$8008 ; the presents-done flag $BD0B = 0: the first presents page plays in full
$8010 ; $BD0B = $FF after it

$8013 c GameRestart ; IM 2 on, the menu, a new game, the first aim; falls into SoundLoop
> Entered from GameStart and from BreakRestart (game 2 entries at frames 6060 and 9091; w1 2; m1 3). DI, I = $FE, IM 2; fills Im2Table $FE00-$FF00 with $FC and writes EI, JP Im2Handler at Im2Jump $FCFC, so every interrupt runs Im2Handler whatever the data bus holds (c1 stage 6, frame 6061). EI, then the menu $B22E (returns when "begin" is chosen), the table $95DA, A = 4 / 3 for $9DC3, the shots left $9F4D from $9BE6, $9D6B, $9B30 = 1, $9520, $9AD9, $9C7B, the racking $9F69 and the first aim StartAim. Then SoundRequest = 0 and EI, into SoundLoop.
$8037 ; the menu, until "begin"
$8061 ; the aim tasks, the cursor and the power bar

$8069 c SoundLoop ; The foreground: wait for BREAK or a sound request, play it on the beeper
> The whole foreground of play (w1 6 290 430 passes, frames 877-11 615). Loops reading InputState $827C (bit 5 BREAK: BreakRestart) and SoundRequest $827D (0: none). A request 1, 2 or 3 picks B = the half period in passes of SoundDelay (37 T a pass) and C = the number of cycles: 1 = 20 / 20 (a ball against a ball, written at $8993), 2 = 30 / 12 (a cushion, $8DC0 / $8DF4 / $8E28), 3 = 250 / 6 (a pocket, $8C17). Each cycle is OUT ($FE) $17 (speaker bit 4 on, border 7), the delay, OUT $07 (off), the delay. At the end it clears SoundRequest itself.
> The tone stops at once when a task sets SoundRequest to 0 while it plays (SoundAbort: border back to 7): StopShotTasks $8483 and ShotReset $84C7 clear it. A new non-zero request during a tone does not restart it; the tone runs on and its final clear drops the new request. The interrupt stays on while it plays, so each half period also holds the time of any task run in it. w1: 1962 cycles played; SoundAbort taken 4 times.
> A request written while a tone plays is lost (SoundDelay tests only for zero; the tone's end clears it); of two writes in one tick the last wins. w1: 178 requests, 141 tones, 21 lost, 16 replaced (docs 9.3).
$806C SoundTestBreak ; BREAK?
$8071 SoundWait ; no request: loop
$8077 ; request 1: B = 20, C = 20
$807D ; request 2: B = 30, C = 12
$8083 ; request 3: B = 250, C = 6
$8086 SoundCycle ; one cycle: speaker on, delay, off, delay
$8098 ; the tone is done: clear the request

$809D c SoundDelay ; Half a period: B passes of 37 T, aborted when the request goes to 0
> Input B (saved). Each pass reads SoundRequest; at 0 it drops its own return address and SoundLoop's (POP BC twice), sets the border/speaker to $07 and jumps back to SoundLoop. w1: 3922 calls (two per cycle).
$809E SoundDelayLoop
$80A8 SoundAbort ; the request was cleared: speaker off, back to the loop

$80B0 c BreakRestart ; BREAK: clear the tasks, reset the stack, back to the menu
> Jumped to from SoundLoop when InputState bit 5 is set (game frame 9091, w1 14 233, f1 5196, p2 4239, v1 2133; m1 at 6830 and 7998). ClearTasks $91CF, SP = $D000, JP GameRestart.

$80B9 c InstallTasks ; Add every task of a list: (period, routine) triples, $FF ends
> DE = the list (AimTasks $84AA, MovingTasks $84BA, the editor's $C518). For each triple: C = the period, HL = the routine, AddTask $9188. Returns at the $FF. w1: 42 lists installed (231 triples read).

$80CC c RemoveTasks ; Remove every task of a list by its routine's address
> DE = the list as for InstallTasks; RemoveTask $91AD for each routine (the period is skipped). w1: 42 lists removed.

$80DE c WaitFrameNoTasks ; Run HL with the tasks stopped, then wait for one interrupt
> Writes RET ($C9) over the JP at Im2Jump+1 ($FCFD): the interrupt is then EI; RET, so the scheduler does not run. EI, calls the routine in HL (WaitFrameCall), HALTs for the next interrupt, DI and puts the JP ($C3) back. Callers: the shot end WaitMessages $8451 and $9C0F, the racking $9F69 (w1 58 calls). The routine may HALT by itself (WaitMessagesLoop does, once a frame): every interrupt it waits for is a plain EI; RET. That is why Im2Jump ran 3503 times in c1 against 2577 for Im2Handler (stage 1).
$80E7 ; one more interrupt, then the JP back

$80EF c WaitFrameCall ; JP (HL)
> The CALL target of WaitFrameNoTasks: jumps to the routine in HL, which returns to $80E7.

$80F0 c Im2Handler ; The interrupt: save every register, run the scheduler
> Reached through Im2Jump $FCFC (EI; JP Im2Handler) on every interrupt while the JP is in place (w1 9536 entries, frames 501-16 377). DI, pushes AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, calls the scheduler $9155, pops them, EI, RET. The alternate registers are not saved: no task uses them.
$80FC ; back from the tasks

@module input ; The controls: keyboard, Interface 2, Kempston, cursor; the key table; BREAK
@about ReadInput $8106 runs as a task of the aim (AimTasks $84AA) and of the editor ($C518), once an interrupt. It calls one of four readers by the control choice $B8BD (0 keyboard, 1 Interface 2, 2 Kempston, 3 cursor joystick; set by the menu), each returning A with bit 0 right, bit 1 left, bit 2 up, bit 3 down, bit 4 fire; then ReadBreak adds bit 5 for CAPS SHIFT + SPACE and stores the byte in InputState $827C, which the aim tasks, the editor and SoundLoop read.
@about The game never reads port $FE for the keys directly but in KeyDown $822E (one key by its character, through the key table) and ReadBreak; the cursor joystick also uses the ROM's KEY-SCAN and K-TEST for "any other key is fire".
$8106 c ReadInput ; The input task: A = the directions and fire of the chosen control, into InputState
> Pushes ReadBreak as the return, then jumps through InputHandlers by the control choice $B8BD (0-3). Output: InputState $827C (bits 0 right, 1 left, 2 up, 3 down, 4 fire, 5 BREAK). Witness: w1 6407 calls (keyboard only), m1 927 (keyboard 317, Kempston 51 at frames 4922-4980, cursor 559 at frames 6272-6830), j1 (Kempston 1087 at frames 622-2185, Interface 2 836 at frames 2503-3540, a shot played with each).
$8116 InputHandlerGot ; HL = InputHandlers[choice]

$811B w InputHandlers 1 ; The four readers by the control choice $B8BD
> Read by ReadInput: $811B/$811C in every build, $811D/$811E in j1, $811F/$8120 in m1 and j1, $8121/$8122 in m1.
$811B ; 0: the keyboard, the three keys of the menu
$811D ; 1: Interface 2
$811F ; 2: Kempston
$8121 ; 3: the cursor joystick (keys 5-8, any other key fire)

$8123 c ReadKeyboard ; The keyboard: the three defined keys, left, right, fire
> The keys are the characters at $B8BE (left), $B8BF (right) and $B8C0 (fire), set by "reconfigure keys" (default m, symbol shift, space: the menu's last line). KeyDown for each: left gives bit 1, right bit 0, fire bit 4; there is no up and down on the keyboard (the cursor runs round the cushions with left and right alone, AimCursorMove $8546). w1: 6407 calls.
$8132 KeyboardRight
$8140 KeyboardFire
$814F KeyboardDone

$8151 c ReadInterface2 ; Interface 2: both joysticks, keys 6-0 and 1-5
> j1 (menu I): 836 calls at frames 2503-3540; key 6 held 40 frames from 2565 moved the cursor, key 0 held 30 frames from 2610 played the shot at 2640. Reads port $EFFE (keys 6-0, Interface 2's joystick 1: 6 left, 7 right, 8 down, 9 up, 0 fire) and port $F7FE (keys 1-5, joystick 2: 1 left, 2 right, 3 down, 4 up, 5 fire), each inverted with CPL, and ORs both into the result bits (fire $10, right $01, left $02, up $04, down $08), so either joystick works.
$8157 ; the 6-0 half row, inverted: a bit set = pressed
$8160 If2Right
$8166 If2Left
$816C If2Down
$8172 If2Up
$8178 If2Stick2 ; the 1-5 half row
$8180 ; the first half row's bits back in A
$8187 If2Stick2Right
$818D If2Stick2Left
$8193 If2Stick2Down
$8199 If2Stick2Up
$819F If2Done

$81A0 c ReadKempston ; Kempston: IN ($1F), active high
> IN A,($1F): bit 0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up, 4 fire, moved to the game's bits (down to bit 3, up to bit 2). m1: 51 calls at frames 4922-4980 (option j on the menu, no stick held); j1 (menu J): 1087 calls at frames 622-2185, the stick left 40 frames from 1000 moved the cursor, fire 30 frames from 1045 played the shot at 1075.
$81AB KempstonRight
$81B1 KempstonLeft
$81B7 KempstonDown
$81BD KempstonUp
$81C3 KempstonDone

$81C4 c ReadCursorKeys ; The cursor joystick: 8 right, 5 left, 7 up, 6 down, any other key fire
> KeyDown for '8', '5', '6', '7'; when none is down, the ROM's KEY_SCAN and K_TEST: any other key (not 5-8) gives fire ($10). m1: 559 calls at frames 6272-6830, 328 with no key ($8213).
$81D1 CursorKeyLeft
$81DC CursorKeyDown
$81E7 CursorKeyUp
$81F2 CursorKeyDirs ; a direction is down: done
$8210 ; another key: fire
$8213 CursorNoKey ; no key: 0

$8216 c ReadBreak ; BREAK into bit 5, then the result into InputState
> The return of every reader (pushed by ReadInput). CAPS SHIFT (port $FEFE bit 0) and SPACE (port $7FFE bit 0) both down add bit 5 ($20). Stores A into InputState. Taken (bit 5 set): p2 1, v1 1, m1 2 (the BREAKs of those recordings).
$822A StoreInput ; store the input byte

$822E c KeyDown ; Is the key with character A down? Z = down
> Looks A up in KeyTable with CPIR (40 bytes), turns the position into the half row's port high byte (B = $FE, $FD, ... $7F) and the bit (D = $10 down to $01: KeyTable runs from the outer key inwards), reads the port and ANDs the bit: Z when the key is down (the port bit is 0). NZ when the character is not in the table. w1: 19 221 calls.
$823A KeyRowLoop ; five keys a half row: B rotates one row on
$8243 KeyBit ; the key's bit in D
$8249 KeyBitLoop
$8250 KeyRead ; read the half row

$8254 b KeyTable 5 ; The 40 keys by half row, from the outer key in: the character each one stands for
> Read by KeyDown (CPIR) and by the menu's key-name code. Characters: CAPS SHIFT = $27, ENTER = $0D, SPACE = $20, SYMBOL SHIFT = $18, else the key's capital letter or digit. The rows are the ports $FEFE (CAPS Z X C V), $FDFE (A S D F G), $FBFE (Q W E R T), $F7FE (1 2 3 4 5), $EFFE (0 9 8 7 6), $DFFE (P O I U Y), $BFFE (ENTER L K J H), $7FFE (SPACE SYMBOL M N B). Stage 1's signature candidate at $8240 was this table.
@res tables/KeyTable.bin

$827C b InputState ; The controls this interrupt: bit 0 right, 1 left, 2 up, 3 down, 4 fire, 5 BREAK
> Written by ReadBreak (every pass of ReadInput), cleared by AimCursorReset $85C8 and the editor ($C504). Read by SoundLoop (BREAK), AimPower $8506 (fire), AimCursorMove $8546, the hall-of-fame entry ($AEBF...), the menu ($B2A7...) and the editor ($C11E...).

$827D b SoundRequest ; The sound SoundLoop plays: 0 none, 1 ball, 2 cushion, 3 pocket
> Written by the physics ($8993 = 1, $8DC0 / $8DF4 / $8E28 = 2, $8C17 = 3), cleared by GameRestart, SoundLoop, StopShotTasks, ShotReset and the editor ($C467). See SoundLoop.

@module shot ; The balls in motion: the redraw task, the draw list, the end of a shot, the start of the aim
@about When the shot is played (AimPower $8506 on the release of fire) the aim tasks go and MovingTasks $84BA come in: BallRedraw $827E, the physics $86C1, ShotEnd $8357 and the message task $9CB7, every interrupt. ShotEnd waits until no ball moves, then scores the shot, ends the frame or the life, and starts the next aim with StartAim $8425.
$827E c BallRedraw ; The redraw task: each ball of DrawList, top to bottom, erased and drawn again where it moved
> Task of MovingTasks (period 0; w1 2839 runs, frames 1086-11 615). For the 7 entries of DrawList $869E (sorted by y by BuildDrawList): A = the ball's number (0 = the cue ball), IX = its record, RedrawBall.
$8283 BallRedrawLoop ; the next entry: number, (y), record address

$8297 c RedrawBall ; Erase and redraw one ball if it moved; a potted ball goes to the row at the top
> IX = the ball record ($8A88 + 14n), A = n. Only for a ball with record +13 bit 7 (on the table) and bit 4 (moved): erases the sprite at its old screen position (+10/+11 into $95D8, XOR draw $9548), then, unless bit 5 (potted) is set, computes the new sprite position from x and y ($9F36), stores it in +10/+11 and $95D8, clears bit 4 and draws it again ($9548 XOR). w1 19 873 calls.
> A potted ball (bit 5; RedrawPotted, w1 18 times): +13 = 0 (off the table); the cue ball (n = 0) stays off; ball n >= 1 is put at x = 24(n-1) + 7, y = 27 (integer bytes, fractions 0: the row of potted balls above the table) and drawn there when the message counter $9F50 is 0 or $50.
$82CC RedrawPotted ; potted: off the table; balls 1-6 to the top row
$8300 RedrawPottedDraw

$8304 c BuildDrawList ; DrawList from the ball records, sorted by y
> Writes the 7 entries of DrawList: the ball's number, its y (record +3, the integer byte), the record's address; then an exchange sort by y: pass k (6 passes) compares entry k with each later entry and swaps the two when entry k's y is not smaller ($8334 CP (HL), $8337 JR C), so after pass k the k-th smallest y is in place (equal y swap too); BallRedraw draws the balls from the top of the screen down. Called by ShotReset (w1 3451 calls also from the physics, frames 706-11 615).
$830F DrawListFill ; one entry a ball: number, y, address
$8329 DrawListSortPass ; the sort: DE stays on entry k, HL walks the later entries; swap when k's y is not smaller
$8330 DrawListSortStep
$833D DrawListSwap ; swap two 4-byte entries
$8348 DrawListSortNext

$8357 c ShotEnd ; The end-of-shot task: frame cleared, or all balls still: foul, shots, next aim
> Task of MovingTasks (w1 2860 runs). If no object ball is on the table (record +13 bit 7 clear for balls 1-6): StopShotTasks, WaitMessages, the score $98F9, $9754 when the foul flag $8E80 is set or the cue ball is off; erase the cue ball; $9BB8, the racking $9F69 and StartAim (the next frame; witness w1 frames 1-3 won).
> Otherwise (ShotEndStill) it returns while any ball has a bit of $70 in +13 (moving, moved or potted). When all are still: StopShotTasks and WaitMessages; then, if no foul and the cue ball is on the table: no ball touched ($89BB = 0) is a foul ($8E80 = 1, ShotFoul); a ball touched but none potted (record +12 = 0 for all six) costs a shot ($9F4D - 1; at 0 the same foul flag). Then the score $98F9, the foul / life code $9754 if $8E80 or the cue ball is off, $93FF (put the cue ball back) when the cue ball is off and lives $9F4E are left, $9DC3 with A = 3, and StartAim.
> ScoreTally runs before LoseLife, so a foul pot still scores: v1 frames 3045-3375, ball 5 into pocket 3 in variation 2, 150 paid, then a life lost (docs 10.9).
$8362 ShotEndBallsLeft ; any object ball left on the table?
$8385 ShotEndFoul ; a foul or the cue ball potted
$8388 ShotEndCueOff ; the cue ball off the screen
$839F ShotEndNextFrame ; the next frame: rack and aim
$83A7 ShotEndStill ; any ball still moving: wait
$83AD ShotEndMovingTest
$83DC ShotEndPottedTest ; was a ball potted this shot?
$83E6 ; one shot fewer
$83EF ShotFoul ; the foul flag $8E80 = 1
$83F7 ShotEndScore ; the score of the shot
$840A ShotEndFoulStill
$840D ShotEndCueBack ; the cue ball off: put it back if a life is left
$841E ShotEndAim ; back to the aim

$8425 c StartAim ; The aim: cursor and power reset, AimTasks in, the three periodic HUD tasks
> AimCursorReset, AimPowerReset, InstallTasks AimTasks, then AddTask $96E6 period 20, $970E period 25 and $9CA7 period 25 (with $9B31 / $9B32 = 0). Called at the game's start, after each shot and each frame (w1 21 times, frames 877-11 386).

$8451 c WaitMessages ; Wait with the tasks stopped until the message on show is done
> WaitFrameNoTasks with WaitMessagesLoop. Called by ShotEnd twice and ShotFoul (w1 25 calls).

$8457 c WaitMessagesLoop ; Run the message task once a frame until the message counter allows play
> With the tasks stopped (WaitFrameNoTasks): while the message counter $9F50 is 1-$4E, or is $4F and no ball was potted, or is 0 with the foul flag $8E80 set: HALT, the message task $9CB7, again. Returns when $9F50 >= $50, or 0 with no foul. w1: 345 passes (320 HALTs).
$846D WaitMessagesPotted
$8478 WaitMessagesFoul ; no message: wait only for a foul
$847D WaitMessagesHalt ; one frame, one step of the message

$8483 c StopShotTasks ; Remove MovingTasks and the two HUD tasks, silence the sound
> RemoveTasks MovingTasks; RemoveTask $96E6 and $970E, each run once more with $9B31 / $9B32 = $FF (their final state); SoundRequest = 0. w1 21 calls.
> The clear ($84A6): 4 of its 21 clears in w1 come while a pocket tone plays (frames 2917, 3895, 6745, 10068): the tone is frozen with the foreground until the end-of-shot sequence returns, then aborted ($80A8; docs 9.3).

$84AA b AimTasks 3 ; The aim's tasks, period 0 each: power, power bar, cursor move, cursor draw, input
> (period, routine) triples, $FF ends. Installed by StartAim, removed by AimPower when the shot is played. AimPower $8506, the power bar $934D, AimCursorMove $8546, AimCursorDraw $85EB, ReadInput $8106.
$84B9 ; the end

$84BA b MovingTasks 3 ; The tasks of the balls in motion, period 0 each: redraw, physics, shot end, messages
> Installed by AimPower ($8532), removed by StopShotTasks. BallRedraw $827E, the physics $86C1, ShotEnd $8357, the message task $9CB7.
$84C6 ; the end

$84C7 c ShotReset ; Before a shot: clear the shot's flags, the velocities and friction steps of the 7 balls
> Called by AimPower on the release of fire (w1 21). Clears $89BB (a ball was touched), the foul flag $8E80, the message counter $9F50, SoundRequest and $8B4C; for each ball record +4..+9 (velocities and friction steps) and +12, and +13 down to bit 7; then BuildDrawList.
$84E1 ShotResetBall

@module aim ; The aim: the power while fire is held, the cursor that runs round the cushions, their variables
@about The cursor is a point on the cushion line: CursorX $86BA 3..252 and CursorY $86BB 51..180. AimCursorDraw turns it into one coordinate round the rectangle (0 at the top left, clockwise: top 0-249, right 249-378, bottom 378-627, left 627-756), adds the signed speed CursorSpeed and turns it back, so left and right alone (or the four directions) run it round the table. The speed grows by one every 10 interrupts while a direction is held, up to 6.
$8506 c AimPower ; The power task: +2 a tick while fire is held; on the release, play the shot
> Task of AimTasks (w1 4425 runs). Fire held (InputState bit 4): Power + 2, back to 0 when it reaches $86 (the bar fills and starts again). Fire up with Power 0: nothing. Fire up with Power > 0 (AimShoot): erase the cursor ($9371), RemoveTasks AimTasks, RemoveTask $9CA7, ShotReset, the shot's velocity from the cursor and the power ($8A02, the ROM calculator) and InstallTasks MovingTasks. w1: 21 shots.
$8517 AimPowerStore
$851B AimShoot ; fire released: shoot if there is power
$8532 ; the balls move

$8539 c AimPowerReset ; Power = 0 and the bar erased
> B = the old Power for $935C (the bar), Power and PowerHigh = 0. Called by StartAim (w1 27).

$8546 c AimCursorMove ; The cursor task: the direction and speed from the controls
> Task of AimTasks and of the editor (w1 4404 runs). When InputState changed since CursorLastInput: right (bit 0) gives +1 on the upper half (CursorY < $72) and -1 on the lower; left the opposite; up (bit 2) +1 on the left half (CursorX < $80), down +1 on the right half; no direction: speed 0. A new direction against the current sign restarts at +-1; the same one keeps accelerating (AimCursorAccel): every 10 counts of CursorAccel, |speed| + 1 up to 6.
$8565 CursorMoveLeft ; left
$8573 CursorMoveUp ; up
$8581 CursorMoveDown ; down
$858F CursorMoveStop ; nothing held: stop
$8592 CursorForward ; along the cushion's coordinate: +1 (or accelerate)
$859D CursorBack ; -1 (or accelerate)
$85A8 CursorNewDirection ; a new direction: speed +-1, countdown 10
$85AD AimCursorAccel ; held: count down, then |speed| + 1 up to 6
$85BD CursorAccelLimit
$85C6 CursorAccelDown

$85C8 c AimCursorReset ; The cursor back to the right cushion, a little at random, and drawn
> InputState and CursorLastInput = 0; CursorX = 252, CursorY = 115, or 114 / 116 by bits 6 and 0 of the R register; speed 0; draws the cursor ($9371 XOR). Called by StartAim (w1 21).
$85E1 CursorResetStore

$85EB c AimCursorDraw ; The cursor draw task: move the cursor round the cushions by its speed
> Task of AimTasks and the editor (w1 4424 runs). Speed 0: nothing. Else erases the cursor ($9371), turns (CursorX, CursorY) into the perimeter coordinate HL, adds the signed speed modulo 756 ($2F4) and turns HL back; the return goes to $9371, which draws it at the new place.
$8609 CursorOnBottom ; on the bottom cushion: 378 + (252 - x)
$8617 CursorOnRight ; on the right cushion: 249 + (y - 51)
$8627 CursorOnLeft ; on the left cushion: 627 + (180 - y)
$862F CursorAddOffset
$8636 CursorOnTop ; on the top cushion: x - 3
$863B CursorAddSpeed ; + the speed, modulo 756
$8646 CursorSpeedSigned
$8651 CursorWrapHigh
$8657 CursorToLeft ; back to x, y: the left cushion?
$866A CursorToBottom ; the bottom?
$867E CursorToRight ; the right?
$8692 CursorToTop ; the top

$869E b DrawList 4 ; The draw list: 7 entries of 4 bytes, then the cursor and the power
> Written by BuildDrawList, read by BallRedraw: the ball's number, its y (the integer byte, the sort key), the record's address (2 bytes). Stage 1's "5 bytes an entry" was wrong: 7 x 4 = 28 bytes, $869E-$86B9.
$86BA CursorX ; the cursor's x on the cushion line, 3..252 (read with CursorY as a word)
$86BB CursorY ; the cursor's y, 51..180
$86BC CursorSpeed ; the signed speed round the cushions, -6..6 (0 at rest)
$86BD CursorAccel ; the countdown to the next +1 of the speed (10)
$86BE CursorLastInput ; InputState at the cursor task's last change
$86BF Power ; the shot's power, 0..$84 in steps of 2 (read with PowerHigh as the word HL)
$86C0 PowerHigh ; 0: the high byte of the power word

@module physics ; The physics task: the motion step, rolling friction, ball against ball
@about The task PhysicsTask $86C1 (list $84BA, period 0: once an interrupt while the balls move) walks the seven ball records BallRecords $8A88 and calls MoveBall $8701 for every ball that is on the table, moving and not potted (+13 bits 7 and 6 set, bit 5 clear).
@about Numbers: a position is 8.8 fixed point in screen pixels (+0 the fraction, +1 the pixel), a velocity 8.8 two's complement in pixels a tick (+4/+5 vx, +6/+7 vy), a friction step a signed byte in 1/256 pixel a tick, added to the velocity every tick (+8 x, +9 y). One tick = one run of the task = one interrupt.
@about MoveBall adds the velocity to the position, clamps it to x 7..248, y 55..176, runs the ball-ball test BallContacts $87EF and the cushion and pocket test PocketOrCushion $8B4E, then applies the friction steps; a component whose sign the step would flip becomes 0, and a ball with both components 0 stops (bit 6 cleared).
@about Ball against ball uses no trigonometry: the whole-pixel |dx|, |dy| of two balls index ContactTable $DBFA (inside / contact ring / apart), the ball is stepped back along its velocity until it lies on the ring, and the ring cell picks one of 31 CollisionRecords $DE3A: three coefficients nx^2, ny^2, |nx ny| (0.8 fixed point, 1.0 = $0100) by which ScaleVelocity $89C4 projects the relative velocity onto the line of centres. The projection is added to one ball and taken from the other: an equal-mass elastic collision.
$86C1 c PhysicsTask ; The physics task: move every rolling ball one tick
> A task of the list $84BA (the balls moving, period 0) and of the editor's list $C518, reached through the scheduler's JP (HL), so no CALL counts it: the first instruction ran game 290, w1 2860, f1 742, p2 1180, v1 822, m1 313 times (frames game 6447-8658, w1 1085-11615).
> Clears EventCount $8B4D, then for each of the seven records (IX from BallRecords $8A88, 14 bytes a step, B = 7..1) calls MoveBall $8701 when +13 has bit 7 (on the table) and bit 6 (moving) set and bit 5 (potted) clear: 2030 records looked at and 286 moves in game, 20020 / 5644 in w1.
> Then advances FrictionTurn $8B4C (0..6, wrapping: $86F2 ran 41 of 290 times in game, 398 of 2860 in w1), so each ball gets its friction steps recomputed once in seven ticks. If EventCount reached 7 the whole pass runs again ($86FB); no recording took that jump (the counts of $86C1 and $86FD are equal in every build). Ends with CALL $8304.
> In w1 the break has 159 physics ticks in the 168 video frames 1085-1252: tick 1 runs at the end of the shot set-up's interrupt (entered at 1079, ends in 1085); nine interrupts are lost, video frames 1155, 1157, ..., 1171, while all seven balls roll (ticks 70-78): each such pass runs past the next INT (work/docs/vpD/w1-sound.json irq; docs 10.1). The samples 1156, 1158, ..., 1172 repeat the one before (tools/physics_model.py).
$86CB PhysicsBallLoop ; IX = the record, B = 7 - the ball number
$86E3 PhysicsNextBall
$86F3 PhysicsTurnStore ; FrictionTurn = (FrictionTurn + 1) mod 7
$86FB ; EventCount >= 7: one more pass (never taken in any recording)
$8701 c MoveBall ; One tick of one ball: move, collide, pocket or bounce, friction, redraw flag
> Input IX = the ball's record, B = 7 - the ball number (0 cue, 1-6); called by PhysicsTask $86C1 (and from the editor $C20B, a path m1 never took). Runs game 286, w1 5644, f1 1574, p2 3850, v1 2299, m1 970 times.
> 1. When FrictionTurn $8B4C = B - 1 the friction steps are recomputed first (FrictionSteps $90F5): one ball a tick in turn.
> 2. x += vx, y += vy (16-bit 8.8 adds). x is clamped to $0700..$F800 (7.0..248.0) with the carry of the add caught (a wrap past 0 or $FFFF goes to the limit); y to $3700..$B000 (55.0..176.0), the low side without the carry test. The clamps fired w1 7 / 9, f1 1 / 2, p2 2 / 0, v1 2 / 2 times ($8738 / $8768); the limits lie past the pocket line, so a clamped ball is potted in the same move.
> 3. Clears +13 bit 4, then BallContacts $87EF (ball against ball) and PocketOrCushion $8B4E (A = the ball number).
> 4. Friction: vx += the sign-extended +8, vy += +9; if the sum crosses zero the component becomes 0 ($8795: w1 61, $87B9: w1 58 times). Both components 0: +13 bit 6 cleared, the ball stops ($87D2: game 4, w1 53).
> 5. Redraw flag: +13 bit 4 is set unless the pixel x, y equal +10, +11. Those hold the sprite's corner (x - 7, y - 7), so the test can only match after a 7-pixel jump; in every build $87EA (set) ran exactly as often as $87DB (the test reached), so every moving ball is redrawn every tick.
$872B MoveXLeft ; vx negative: low clamp $0700
$8738 MoveXClamp ; x = the limit in DE
$8739 MoveXStore
$875D MoveYUp ; vy negative: low clamp $3700, no carry test
$8768 MoveYClamp
$8769 MoveYStore
$8787 FrictionVx ; DE = the step, sign-extended
$8793 FrictionVxCheck ; carry here = vx crossed zero
$8798 FrictionVxStore
$879E FrictionVy
$87AB FrictionVyAdd
$87B7 FrictionVyCheck
$87BC FrictionVyStore
$87C2 StopTest ; vx = vy = 0: RES 6, the ball stops
$87D6 RedrawTest ; compares +1 with +10 and +3 with +11 (the sprite corner, x - 7): never equal in practice
$87EA RedrawSet
$87EF c BallContacts ; Ball against ball: find a touching ball, step back onto the contact ring, exchange velocities
> Input IX = the moving ball. Runs as often as MoveBall (game 286, w1 5644). For every record IY on the table (7 looked at, the ball itself included; it and any ball on the same pixel are skipped by dx = dy = 0): whole-pixel |dx|, |dy| (the +1, +3 bytes) both below 16, then ContactTable $DBFA[16 |dy| + |dx|]. A negative entry ($80) means overlap (w1 201, f1 55, p2 124, v1 80, m1 47 times, $8833); 0 (apart) and a ring entry without overlap are no contact (RET P).
> On overlap: StepBack $8E33 moves IX back along its own velocity by less than a pixel and the table is read again until the entry is no longer negative; ContactSigns $89B5 = 2 (dx < 0) + (dy < 0) with dx = IX - IY. The entry n (1..31) selects CollisionRecords $DE3A + 6 (n - 1), kept in ContactRecordPtr $89B7 with IY in ContactOther $89B9; the loop goes on, so the last touching ball wins. The ring entry was found w1 71, f1 28, p2 49, v1 32, m1 17 times; game (c1, a game of fouls) never had a ball hit.
> The exchange ($889C) runs if the ball moves towards the other in x or y (sign of dx against the sign of vx, the same for y); it always did when a contact was found (the JP P to $89AA never taken). With dv = v(IY) - v(IX) and the record's w0 = nx^2, w1 = ny^2, w2 = |nx ny|: A = w0 dvx, B = w2 dvy, C = w2 dvx, D = w1 dvy (ScaleVelocity $89C4, 4 calls a contact). The sign s of nx ny is + when dx and dy have the same sign (ContactSigns 0 or 3), - otherwise. IX.v += (A + sB, D + sC); IY.v -= the same. The other ball is set moving (+13 bit 6), BallHitFlag $89BB = 1, the sound request $827D = 1, EventCount += 2, and both balls get new friction steps (FrictionSteps $90F5 twice).
> Finally the moving ball's fractions are set to $80 (the centre of its pixel), also when it was moving away.
$87FB ContactLoop ; PUSH ContactNext as the return address of every test below
$880F ContactDy
$881D ContactLookup ; A = 16 |dy| + |dx|
$8833 ContactBackOff ; step back, recompute signed dx, dy and the signs
$8849 ContactBackDy
$885E ContactBackLookup ; still negative: step back again
$886F ; the ring entry n: record = CollisionRecords + 6 (n - 1)
$8887 ContactNext ; the next IY (reached by RET from the tests)
$8891 ; all seen: ContactFound zero = no contact
$889C ; moving towards the other ball in x or in y: exchange
$8912 ExchangeVx ; A = 0: same-sign quadrant (add the cross terms), else subtract
$8928 ExchangeVxAdd
$8929 ExchangeVxStore
$8945 ExchangeVyAdd
$8946 ExchangeVyStore
$8964 ExchangeOtherVxAdd
$8965 ExchangeOtherVxStore
$8983 ExchangeOtherVyAdd
$8984 ExchangeOtherVyStore
$89AA ContactCentre ; fractions of x and y = $80
$89B2 ContactDone
$89B5 b ContactVars ; The ball-contact work variables
> Written and read by BallContacts $87EF (w1 18 997 reads over the block). BallHitFlag $89BB is cleared at the shot's start ($84C8) and read by the end-of-shot task ($83CD) to tell whether any ball was hit.
$89B5 ContactSigns ; 2 (dx < 0) + (dy < 0), dx = mover - other, recomputed on each back-off step
$89B6 ContactSignsFound ; the signs at the contact found
$89B7 ContactRecordPtr ; the low byte of the CollisionRecords pointer
$89B8 ContactFound ; the pointer's high byte ($DE), cleared before the scan: non-zero = a contact found
$89B9 ContactOther ; the other ball's record (IY), a word
$89BB BallHitFlag ; 1 = a ball was hit during this shot
$89BC ExchangeA ; w0 dvx, 8.8 signed
$89BE ExchangeB ; w2 dvy
$89C0 ExchangeC ; w2 dvx
$89C2 ExchangeD ; w1 dvy
$89C4 c ScaleVelocity ; HL = DE x the coefficient HL (0.8 fixed point, $0100 = 1.0), signed
> Input HL = a coefficient of a collision record (H = 1: exactly 1.0, else 0 and L the fraction), DE = a signed 8.8 velocity; output HL = the product, 8.8 signed; uses A, BC, DE. Coefficient 1.0 returns DE at once ($89C7: 24 of 284 calls in w1). Otherwise |DE| is formed, the fraction times the low byte (MulByte $89F5) rounded to its high byte, plus the fraction times the high byte, and the sign put back. Runs only on contacts: w1 284, f1 112, p2 196, v1 124, m1 68 calls (4 a contact), never in game.
$89D5 ScaleFrac ; fraction x the low byte, rounded
$89E2 ScaleInt ; + fraction x the integer byte
$89F5 c MulByte ; HL += A x DE, 8 bits of A from bit 0
> Shift-and-add: eight steps, A rotated right, DE added when the bit is set, DE doubled each step. Called twice by ScaleVelocity $89C4 (w1 520 calls).
$89F7 MulByteLoop
$89FB MulByteShift

@module shotsetup ; The shot: direction and speed through the ROM calculator
@about AimShot $8A02 turns the aim cursor and the power into the cue ball's velocity with the ROM's floating-point calculator (RST $28): dx = cursor x - cue x, dy = cursor y - cue y, r = SQR (dx*dx + dy*dy), then vy = 16 P dy / r and vx = 16 P dx / r with P the power (at most 126), so the speed is P / 16 pixels a tick (at most 7.9) towards the cursor. The ROM needs IY = ERR_NR and the I register's ROM value $3F while it runs; the routine puts I = $FE back.
$8A02 c AimShot ; The shot: the cue ball's velocity from the cursor, the cue ball and the power
> Called by the power task $8506 when the shot is played: game 4, w1 21, f1 9, p2 6, v1 4, m1 1 times (one a shot; frames game 6441-8491, w1 1079-11557). IY = $5C3A, I = $3F, SET-MIN clears the calculator stack. Stacks the cursor x CursorX $86BA and the cue ball's pixel x $8A89 (STACK-A, whole pixels: the fractions are not used) and runs AimCalcDx $8A1A.
$8A1A b AimCalcDx ; RST $28 literals: dx and dx*dx
> Read by the ROM's calculator after the RST $28 at $8A19 (5 bytes, game 20 reads = 4 shots). Stack in: cursor x, cue x. Out: dx, dx*dx.
$8A1A ; $03 subtract: dx = cursor x - cue x
$8A1B ; $31 duplicate, $31 duplicate
$8A1D ; $04 multiply: dx, dx*dx
$8A1E ; $38 end-calc
$8A1F c AimStackDy ; Stack the cursor y and the cue ball's y, then r and dy / r
> The calculator returns here. Stacks CursorY $86BB and the cue ball's pixel y $8A8B, runs AimCalcDyOverR $8A2D.
$8A2D b AimCalcDyOverR ; RST $28 literals: dy, r = SQR (dx*dx + dy*dy), dy / r
> 16 bytes read once a shot (game 64 reads). Stack in: dx, dx*dx, cursor y, cue y. Out: dx, r, dy / r. Memory 0 of the calculator holds dy.
$8A2D ; $03 subtract: dy; $C0 st-mem-0 (dy); $01 exchange: dx, dy, dx*dx
$8A30 ; $E0 get-mem-0, $31 duplicate, $04 multiply, $0F addition: dx, dy, dx*dx + dy*dy
$8A34 ; $28 sqr: dx, dy, r
$8A35 ; $01 exchange, $C0 st-mem-0 (dy), $02 delete: dx, r
$8A38 ; $31 duplicate, $E0 get-mem-0, $01 exchange: dx, r, dy, r
$8A3B ; $05 division: dx, r, dy / r; $38 end-calc
$8A3D c AimStoreVy ; vy = ScaleByPower (dy / r), then dx / r
> Stores BC from ScaleByPower $8A5D in the cue ball's vy $8A8E, then runs AimCalcDxOverR $8A45.
$8A45 b AimCalcDxOverR ; RST $28 literals: $05 division (dx / r), $38 end-calc
$8A47 c AimStoreVx ; vx = ScaleByPower (dx / r); the cue ball moves
> Stores BC in the cue ball's vx $8A8C, sets its +13 bit 6 (moving), I = $FE (the game's IM 2 table again), and jumps to FrictionSteps $90F5 with IX = the cue ball.
$8A5D c ScaleByPower ; BC = 16 P x the top of the calculator stack, as a 16-bit two's complement 8.8
> P = Power $86BF, values of $80 and more taken as $7E (the power only grows in steps of 2, so at most 126); BC = 16 P (2 P shifted 3 times) stacked by STACK-BC, then ScaleByPowerCalc $8A7A, then FP-TO-BC through ScaleToBC $8A85. Runs twice a shot (game 8, w1 42).
$8A66 ScalePowerOk
$8A7A b ScaleByPowerCalc ; RST $28 literals: multiply, and add 65536 to a value that is not positive
> 11 bytes; the jump-true skips the stk-data and the addition for a positive product. A negative x + 65536 is its 16-bit two's complement, which FP-TO-BC returns in BC. A product of exactly 0 (the cursor on the cue ball's row or column) also gets 65536 added; FP-TO-BC then reads the float's bytes as an integer, BC = 0 with the carry set, so the component is 0 after all (known only from the ROM's code, see the notes).
$8A7A ; $04 multiply: 16 P x (d / r); $31 duplicate; $37 greater-0
$8A7D ; $00 jump-true, offset 6: to the end-calc at $8A84 when positive
$8A7F ; $34 stk-data: $00 (one mantissa byte, exponent next), $41 (+$50 = $91), $00: 65536
$8A83 ; $0F addition; $38 end-calc
$8A85 c ScaleToBC ; JP FP-TO-BC: the value off the calculator stack into BC
> Jumps to the ROM's FP-TO-BC $2DA2, which returns to AimStoreVy $8A3D or AimStoreVx $8A47.

@module balls ; The ball records, the other player's records, the physics counters
@about Fourteen 14-byte records: the seven balls of the player at the table (cue ball first, then balls 1-6) and the same seven of the other player in a two-player game, swapped as one 98-byte block on the turn change (the swap loop $9C5B in $9C0F, p2 98 bytes once). Then two counters of the physics task.
$8A88 b BallRecords 14 ; The seven ball records of the player at the table: cue ball, balls 1-6
> 14 bytes a ball: +0/+1 x and +2/+3 y, 8.8 fixed point in screen pixels, fraction first, the centre of the ball; +4/+5 vx and +6/+7 vy, 8.8 two's complement in pixels a tick; +8 / +9 the friction steps x / y, signed bytes in 1/256 pixel a tick (FrictionSteps $90F5, negated by the bounces $8DCB / $8DFF, swapped by $8D7D); +10/+11 the screen x / y of the sprite as last drawn (x - 7, y - 7: the corner of the 16 x 16 ball); +12 the pocket the ball fell into (1 top left, 2 top middle, 3 top right, 4 bottom right, 5 bottom middle, 6 bottom left, written by PocketStore $8BE3, 0 = none, cleared at each shot by $84C7); +13 flags.
> +13: bit 7 on the table, bit 6 moving, bit 5 potted (fallen, waiting for the redraw $8297 to move it to the row of potted balls and clear the byte), bit 4 to be redrawn. $80 at rest, $D0 rolling (moving and redrawn), $90 = on the table and to be redrawn.
> Positions from $9D5D by $9D2C. Read by every build; the cue ball's x pixel $8A89 most (w1 28 930 reads). +12 of balls 1-6 read 29-42 times in w1 (the end-of-shot test $83DC and the wrong-pocket test $8C37), +12 of the cue ball once in p2 (the turn swap).
$8A88 BallCue ; the cue ball: +0/+1 x
$8A8A ; +2/+3 y
$8A8C ; +4/+5 vx
$8A8E ; +6/+7 vy
$8A90 ; +8 / +9 friction step x / y
$8A92 ; +10 / +11 sprite screen x / y
$8A94 ; +12 pocket, +13 flags
$8A96 BallOne ; ball 1
$8AA4 BallTwo ; ball 2
$8AB2 BallThree ; ball 3
$8AC0 BallFour ; ball 4
$8ACE BallFive ; ball 5
$8ADC BallSix ; ball 6
$8AEA b OtherPlayerBalls 14 ; The other player's seven ball records, same format, swapped with BallRecords on the turn change
> Zero in the image. Every byte read once in p2 only (the swap $9C0F at frame 1738, two players), and +13 of each of the seven read once in every build (game, w1, f1, p2, v1, m1; reader not identified, see the notes). At w1 frame 1060 balls 1-6 here have +10 = 0, 24, ... 120 and +11 = 20: the sprite corners of the row of potted balls ($82CC puts a potted ball at x 24 (n - 1) + 7, y 27). Stage 1 read this as the potted row itself: it is the second player's set.
$8B4C b FrictionTurn ; The ball whose friction steps are recomputed this tick, 0..6
> Advanced mod 7 by PhysicsTask $86C1, compared by MoveBall $8701 with B - 1 (6 - the ball number), cleared at the shot's start ($84C7). Read game 576, w1 8504.
$8B4D b EventCount ; Bounces and contacts in this pass of PhysicsTask: cushion +1, ball +2; 7 or more repeats the pass
> Cleared by PhysicsTask $86C1, raised by ReflectDiagonal $8D7D, ReflectX $8DCB, ReflectY $8DFF (+1) and BallContacts $87EF (+2). Never reached 7 in a recording (the repeat at $86FB not taken).

@module cushions ; The cushions and the pockets
@about Coordinates are the ball centre's whole pixels. The table: cushion lines x = 17 and 238 (left, right), y = 65 and 166 (top, bottom): a ball past one is stepped back along its velocity (StepBack $8E33, less than a pixel a step) until it is on the line, then that velocity component is reversed (ReflectX $8DCB, ReflectY $8DFF). The cushion faces, 7 pixels further out, are at y 58 / 173.
@about Six pockets. The corner pockets are 45-degree channels along y - x = 48 (top left), x + y = 183 (bottom left), x + y = 303 (top right), x - y = 72 (bottom right); a ball in a corner zone with |the offset from that diagonal| of 6 or 7 (large pockets) or 2 or 3 (small) hits the jaw: the bounce on a 45-degree wall swaps vx and vy (ReflectDiagonal $8D7D). The middle pockets are channels between x = 122 and 134 (large) or 125 and 131 (small), with jaw points at x 115 / 140 (large) or 118 / 137 (small) on the cushion faces y 58 and 173, tested with the radius table Table8x8 $DCFA (PointJaw $8CF8).
@about A ball whose centre leaves x 8..247 or y 56..175 is potted: PocketOrCushion $8B4E puts it into its pocket's box, marks it, requests the sound and checks the variations' fouls. The pocket size is the menu option $B8BC (1 large, the image's value; 0 small): CushionsLarge $8FBB or CushionsSmall $8E81, which also patch the depth test of DiagonalJaw $8C7E.
$8B4E c PocketOrCushion ; Pot a ball that left the playing area, else the cushion tests for the pocket size
> Input IX = the ball, A = the ball number (0 cue, 1-6); runs once a move (game 286, w1 5644). Restores DiagonalJaw's shallow exit ($8C92 = RET C, NOP). Inside x 8..247, y 56..175 ($08 <= x < $F8, $38 <= y < $B0): CushionsBySize $8C74.
> Outside, the ball is potted ($8B71: w1 18, f1 3, p2 2, v1 5, never in game or m1): x and y are clamped into the pocket's box, by the column (x < 64, < 192, else) and the row (y < 115 top, else bottom): large boxes x 8-15, 122-133, 240-247 and y 56-63, 168-175; small x 8-11, 125-130, 244-247 and y 56-59, 172-175; fractions 0. +12 = the pocket number (columns 1-3 on the top row, 7 minus that on the bottom: 4 right, 5 middle, 6 left), +13 bits 5 and 4 set and 6 cleared, the sound request $827D = 3.
> Fouls of the variations (an object ball, FoulCode $8E80 still 0, the cue ball on the table, variation $B8C3 not 0): variation 1 ($8C4B) sets FoulCode = 2 if a lower-numbered ball is still on the table (v1, 2 times); variation 2 sets FoulCode = 3 unless the pocket + $9F43 (mod 6, 1-6) is the ball's number ($8C43, v1 2 times).
$8B71 PocketSnap ; outside the playing area: potted
$8B94 PocketLeftColumn ; x < 64: boxes x 8..11 or 8..15
$8B9E PocketRightColumn ; x >= 192: x 244..247 or 240..247
$8BA8 PocketClampX ; C = x clamped to L..H (reached by RET)
$8BAD PocketClampXHigh
$8BB2 PocketClampXKeep
$8BB4 PocketRowSelect ; y < 115: boxes y 56..59 or 56..63
$8BCD PocketBottomRow ; y 172..175 or 168..175
$8BD7 PocketClampY ; B = y clamped
$8BDC PocketClampYHigh
$8BE1 PocketClampYKeep
$8BE3 PocketStore ; the ball in the box; C = the column 1-3
$8BFE PocketNumberBottom ; y >= 128: pocket = 7 - column
$8C06 PocketMark ; +12 = the pocket, +13: potted, redraw, stopped; sound 3
$8C1D ; the foul tests of the variations (object ball, no foul yet, cue on the table, variation set)
$8C37 ; variation 2: the pocket + $9F43 wrapped to 1-6 must be the ball
$8C43 PocketWrongTest
$8C4B PocketOrderTest ; variation 1: a lower ball still on the table is a foul
$8C56 PocketOrderLoop
$8C6C PocketOrderNext
$8C74 c CushionsBySize ; The cushion tests of the pocket size: $B8BC = 0 small, else large
> Runs on every move inside the playing area: CushionsLarge $8FBB in game 286, w1 5626, f1 1571, p2 3848, v1 2294 (the image's $B8BC = 1); CushionsSmall $8E81 only in m1 (970, the small-pockets option).
$8C7E c DiagonalJaw ; A corner pocket's 45-degree jaw: A = the offset from the diagonal (carry = negative), BC = the wall's normal signs
> Entered by JP from the corner tests of CushionsSmall $8E81 / CushionsLarge $8FBB with A = the signed offset of the ball from the pocket's diagonal (carry set when negative), C / B = $00 or $FF for the x / y sign of the wall's normal, HL = the test to repeat. A negative offset is negated and B, C complemented. Offset 2-3 (small) or 6-7 (large): JawContact. Less: inside the channel, no bounce (RET C at $8C92). More: the ball went too deep: $8C92 is patched to JR C,JawContact and StepBack $8E33 runs with HL pushed as its return, so the test repeats from the cushion test until the offset is within the band. Entered w1 43, f1 46, p2 20, v1 31 times; the deep case $8C94 w1 7, f1 4, v1 6.
> JawContact: if the ball moves into the wall in x and in y ($8CE4), or in the one axis whose speed is the larger (|vx| against |vy|, $8CB5), the fractions are set to C, B and ReflectDiagonal $8D7D swaps the components; else ($8CED) the fractions are set to the complements and no bounce.
$8C80 ; negative offset: negate, complement the signs
$8C8A JawDepthTest ; CP 2 / CP 6 here and CP 3 / CP 7 at $8C8E: the operands patched by $8E81 / $8FBB
$8C92 JawShallowExit ; RET C / NOP, patched to JR C,JawContact after a step back
$8C94 ; too deep: patch, step back, repeat the test at HL
$8CA2 JawContact ; L = C xor vx, H = B xor vy: bit 7 = moving into the wall
$8CC9 JawAbsVy
$8CDA JawCompare ; the larger component decides
$8CDF JawCompareSub
$8CE4 JawReflect
$8CED JawAway ; no bounce: fractions = the complements of C, B
$8CF8 c PointJaw ; A middle pocket's jaw point (C, B): the radius test of Table8x8, step back, bounce
> Input IX, C / B = the jaw point's x / y ($73 or $76, $8C or $89; $3A or $AD); entered from the middle-cushion tests. |dx| < 8 and |dy| < 8, and not |dx| = |dy| >= 5, else no contact; then Table8x8 $DCFA[8 |dy| + |dx|]: 0 apart, $80 overlap (StepBack $8E33 and look again, $8D26: w1 1, v1 9), 1 touching. Entered w1 7, v1 3, m1 8 times.
> The bounce uses bit 7 of the point's coordinates in place of the normal's signs (C xor vx, B xor vy, as DiagonalJaw does with its signs): moving into the point in both axes, the fractions set from the position's sign and ReflectDiagonal $8D7D; in x only, ReflectX $8DCB; in y only, ReflectY $8DFF; moving away, nothing.
$8D00 PointJawDy
$8D0C PointJawDyAbs ; |dx| = |dy| >= 5: outside the radius
$8D15 PointJawLookup
$8D26 PointJawBackOff
$8D31 PointJawBackDy
$8D3A PointJawBackLookup
$8D4A PointJawReflect
$8D63 PointJawCorner ; moving into it in both axes
$8D6D PointJawFracY
$8D7A PointJawSetFracY
$8D7D c ReflectDiagonal ; The bounce off a 45-degree wall: swap vx and vy (and the friction steps), negated when B xor C is positive
> Ran w1 10, f1 7, p2 1, v1 5, m1 2 times. (vx, vy, step x, step y) become (vy, vx, step y, step x), all four negated when the normal's signs B and C are equal ($8D95: w1 4, v1 5, m1 1). Sound request $827D = 2, EventCount $8B4D + 1.
$8DAB ReflectDiagonalStore
$8DCB c ReflectX ; The bounce off a side cushion: vx and the x friction step negated
> vx = -vx (NEG / CPL with the carry: a 16-bit negate), +8 negated, the x fraction set to $00 or $FF by the sign of the x pixel byte (bit 7: right half $FF); sound request $827D = 2, EventCount $8B4D + 1. Ran game 1, w1 31, f1 11, p2 24, v1 14, m1 7 times.
$8DEF ReflectXFrac
$8DFF c ReflectY ; The bounce off a top or bottom cushion: vy and the y friction step negated
> As ReflectX $8DCB for y: vy, +9 negated, the y fraction $00 or $FF by bit 7 of the y pixel; sound 2, EventCount + 1. Ran game 1, w1 48, f1 9, p2 39, v1 19, m1 8 times.
$8E23 ReflectYFrac
$8E33 c StepBack ; Move the ball back along its velocity by less than one pixel
> Input IX. Copies vx, vy, halves both (arithmetic shifts) until each lies in -255..255 / 256 (high byte 0, or $FF with a non-zero low byte), then subtracts them from x and y. Called by BallContacts $87EF, DiagonalJaw $8C7E, PointJaw $8CF8 and the cushion tests: game 2, w1 401, f1 106, p2 281, v1 173, m1 79 calls.
$8E40 StepBackNormalise
$8E4B StepBackCheckVy
$8E56 StepBackHalve
$8E60 StepBackApply
$8E80 b FoulCode ; The shot's foul from the variations: 0 none, 2 wrong order, 3 wrong pocket
> Written by PocketOrCushion $8B4E ($8C47 = 3, $8C68 = 2: v1 only), cleared at the shot's start ($84CB); read by the end-of-shot code ($8375, $83BD, $83F1, $83FA, $8478) and $8C1D: game 310, w1 2929 reads.
$8E81 c CushionsSmall ; The cushions and the pocket mouths for small pockets
> Patches DiagonalJaw's depth band to 2-3 ($8C8B = 2, $8C8F = 3), then by x: x >= 239 the right end, x < 17 the left end, else the long cushions. Ends: the straight cushion (y 67..163) reflects vx once x is back within 17..238; above y 67 / below y 164 a corner pocket: DiagonalJaw on y - x = 48 / x + y = 183 (left) or x + y = 303 / x - y = 72 (right). Long cushions: y < 65 the top, y >= 167 the bottom, else free. On those: x < 19 or x >= 236 back to the ends; x 125..130 the pocket channel (no test); x 118..124 and 131..137 the jaw zone: PointJaw at (118 or 137, 58 / 173) until the ball is past the face, then x = 125.0 / 130.996 and ReflectX (the channel walls); elsewhere the straight cushion: step back to y = 65 / 166 and ReflectY. Only m1 (small pockets) ran it: 970 times, frames 7430-7745.
$8E8B SmallCushionTest ; the test repeated after each step back (HL)
$8E97 SmallLeftEnd
$8EB4 SmallTopLeftJaw ; offset = y - 48 - x
$8EBE SmallBottomLeftJaw ; offset = 183 - y - x
$8EC8 SmallRightEnd
$8EE5 SmallTopRightJaw ; offset = y - 47 + x, mod 256
$8EF1 SmallBottomRightJaw ; offset = 184 - y + x, mod 256
$8EFD SmallLongCushions
$8F07 SmallBottomCushion
$8F28 SmallBottomLeftOfPocket ; jaw x 118, channel wall 125.0
$8F33 SmallBottomStraight ; y >= 174 here: the ball put on the jaw point
$8F43 SmallBottomChannel ; past the face: on the channel wall, ReflectX
$8F54 SmallBottomLine ; step back to y 166, ReflectY
$8F61 SmallTopCushion
$8F82 SmallTopLeftOfPocket
$8F8D SmallTopStraight
$8F9D SmallTopChannel
