$8FBB c CushionsLarge ; The cushions and the pocket mouths for large pockets (the default)
> As CushionsSmall $8E81 with the large geometry: the band 6-7 ($8C8B = 6, $8C8F = 7), the corner zones above y 71 and below y 160, the long cushions' ends at x 23 / 232, the middle channel x 122..133 with the walls 122.0 / 133.996, the jaw zones x 115..121 and 134..140 with the jaw points (115 or 140, 58 / 173). The same cushion lines x 17 / 238, y 65 / 166. Ran game 286, w1 5626, f1 1571, p2 3848, v1 2294 times; the straight-cushion bounces are the bulk of ReflectX / ReflectY.
$8FC5 LargeCushionTest ; the test repeated after each step back (HL)
$8FD1 LargeLeftEnd
$8FEE LargeTopLeftJaw ; offset = y - 48 - x
$8FF8 LargeBottomLeftJaw ; offset = 183 - y - x
$9002 LargeRightEnd
$901F LargeTopRightJaw ; offset = y - 47 + x, mod 256
$902B LargeBottomRightJaw ; offset = 184 - y + x, mod 256
$9037 LargeLongCushions ; y < 65 top, y >= 167 bottom, else free
$9041 LargeBottomCushion
$9062 LargeBottomLeftOfPocket ; jaw x 115, channel wall 122.0
$906D LargeBottomStraight ; y >= 174: the ball put on the jaw point (never run)
$907D LargeBottomChannel ; past the face: on the channel wall, ReflectX
$908E LargeBottomLine ; step back to y 166, ReflectY
$909B LargeTopCushion
$90BC LargeTopLeftOfPocket
$90C7 LargeTopStraight
$90D7 LargeTopChannel
$90E8 LargeTopLine ; step back to y 65, ReflectY

@module friction ; The rolling friction: the steps from the direction of motion
@about FrictionSteps $90F5 turns a ball's velocity direction into two friction steps from FrictionTable $DD3A: a deceleration of about 5/256 pixel a tick per tick against the motion, split between x and y by the direction, rounded to whole steps.
$90F5 c FrictionSteps ; The friction steps +8 / +9 of the ball IX from the direction of its velocity
> |vx| and |vy| (AbsHL $914A) are shifted left together, at most 8 times, until bit 3 of either high byte is set: two 4-bit numbers in the ratio of the components (high nibbles of the 8.8 values after normalising; small speeds use the shifted-up low bytes). FrictionTable $DD3A[16 ny + nx]: the low nibble is the x step, the high nibble the y step, each negated when its velocity component is positive, so the step opposes the motion (0 for a zero component). Speeds over 15 pixels a tick would lose their top bits; the shot gives at most 7.9.
> Called by MoveBall $8701 (one ball a tick in turn), BallContacts $87EF (both balls after a contact) and AimStoreVx $8A47 (the shot): game 42, w1 960, f1 284, p2 655, v1 392, m1 174 calls, frames game 6447-8651.
$910A FrictionNormalise
$911C FrictionLookup ; A = 16 ny + nx
$9134 FrictionStoreX ; +8
$9146 FrictionStoreY ; +9
$914A c AbsHL ; HL = |HL|
> Two's complement negate when bit 7 of H is set. Called twice by FrictionSteps $90F5 (game 84, w1 1920).

@module scheduler ; The task scheduler of the interrupt: 25 slots, add, remove, clear
@about A task is a routine with a period: the scheduler calls it when its countdown goes below zero and reloads the countdown with the period, so a period p runs it every p + 1 interrupts (0: every one). A slot whose high byte is 0 is free.
$9155 c RunTasks ; The scheduler: count each slot down, call the routines whose time has come
> Called by Im2Handler on every interrupt (w1 9536 entries). Each of the 25 slots of TaskSlots: skip if free; count +3 down; at -1 call the routine (CallTask, JP (HL)) and reload +3 from +2. Tasks run in slot order (the order they were added). w1: 34 301 task calls.
$915B RunTasksLoop ; the next slot
$9173 ; reload the countdown
$917C RunTasksNext ; 4 bytes a slot

$9187 c CallTask ; JP (HL)

$9188 c AddTask ; Put routine HL with period C into the first free slot
> Countdown 0, so it first runs at the scheduler's next visit of its slot: in the same tick when the slot comes after the caller's (the release: MovingTasks in slots 1-3 run in AimPower's tick), otherwise at the next tick. No free slot: nothing (25 are never all used). w1: 252 calls.
$918E AddTaskLoop
$919F AddTaskFree ; the free slot

$91AD c RemoveTask ; Free the slot of routine HL
> The first slot whose address is HL gets high byte 0. w1: 252 calls.
$91B3 RemoveTaskLoop
$91C4 RemoveTaskNext ; the next slot

$91CF c ClearTasks ; Free all 25 slots (DI while it clears)
> Called by BreakRestart (one a BREAK) and by the editor. Zeros the 100 bytes of TaskSlots.

$91DF b TaskSlots 4 ; 25 task slots: the routine (0 high byte = free), the period, the countdown
> Written by AddTask, RemoveTask, ClearTasks; read by RunTasks every interrupt.

@module tabledraw ; The table, the power bar and its box, the aiming cursor, the XOR line
@about Everything here is drawn with one primitive, XorLine $93B3: a horizontal run of B pixels from (x E, y D), XORed onto the screen, so drawing a figure twice erases it. The power bar and the cursor are redrawn every frame by the aim list's tasks; the table and the power box once per game (NewGameScreen $95DA) and once per editor visit ($C042).
@about No aim line exists: the aim is the cursor itself, a 5 x 5 cross that runs round the cushion line (x 3..252, y 51..180); the cue ball and the object balls are sprites (balldraw).
$9243 c DrawTable ; Draw the table: the outer frame and the six cushions, with gaps for the six pockets
> Called by NewGameScreen $95DA and the editor $C042, once each (game 1 at frame 6186, w1 1 at 626, v1 2, m1 2). Every run is a call of XorLine $93B3 with D = y, E = x, B = length.
> The frame: the top line y 48 and the bottom line y 183, x 0-255 (two runs of 128 each); the left edge x 0 and the right edge x 255, y 49-182 (134 one-pixel runs each).
> The four long cushions, 9 lines each, sloped at 45 degrees on their pocket side: top left y 49-57 from x 15+i to 117, top right from x 138 to 240-i (i = 0..8 the line); bottom left and bottom right the same mirrored, y 182 up to 174. The two side cushions: x 1-9 at the left and x 246-254 at the right, a 9-line slope in (y 63-71, run 1..9), 88 straight lines (y 72-159), a 9-line slope out (y 160-168).
> Large pockets ($B8BC <> 0, the menu's L key; the default in the image): the long cushions start 4 pixels later and run 96 pixels (x 19+i..114, 141..236-i), the side cushions start at y 67 and run 80 straight lines. Small pockets ($B8BC = 0, the S key, m1 frame 7110): the numbers above. The gaps left are the pockets: the four corners and the middle of the top and bottom (x 118-137 small, 115-140 large).
$9260 TableTopLeft ; top left cushion: x+1, y+1, length-1 a line
$927A TableTopRight ; top right cushion: y+1, length-1
$9282 ; y 183, x 0: the bottom line (a y/x pair, not an address)
$928E ; y 182, x 15: the bottom left cushion, going up
$929F TableBottomLeft ; bottom left cushion: y-1, x+1, length-1
$92A8 ; y 182, x 138: the bottom right cushion, going up
$92B9 TableBottomRight ; bottom right cushion: y-1, length-1
$92C8 TableLeftEdge ; the left edge, x 0, y 49-182
$92CF ; y 63, x 1
$92DE TableLeftSlopeIn ; left cushion: run 1..9 from x 1, y 63 (67 large)
$92F2 TableLeftStraight ; 88 lines (80 large) of 9 pixels
$92FD TableLeftSlopeOut ; run 9..1
$930C TableRightEdge ; the right edge, x 255
$9313 ; y 63, x 254
$9322 TableRightSlopeIn ; right cushion: x-1, run+1 from x 254
$9338 TableRightStraight ; 88 / 80 lines of x 246-254
$9343 TableRightSlopeOut ; x+1, run-1
$934D c PowerBarTask ; The aim list's power bar task: erase the bar drawn last frame, draw the bar for the power now
> A task of the aim list $84AA, period 0 (every interrupt while aiming): game 1443 runs, w1 4404, f1 2035, p2 1369, v1 846, m1 316 (also the editor's list $C518). $86BF is the power (grown by $8506 while fire is held), $86C0 the length drawn last time: DrawPowerBar $935C with B = $86C0 (XOR: erases), then with B = $86BF (draws), then $86C0 = $86BF. A power of 0 draws nothing (XorLine returns on B = 0).
$935C c DrawPowerBar ; XOR the power bar: 4 lines of B pixels (capped at 126) from x 4, y 10-13
> Input B = the length. Lengths of 128 and more are drawn as 126 (the power runs 0..$84, so the last three steps $80, $82, $84 look full). The bar lies inside PowerBox $9395 (x 2-131, y 8-15). Called by PowerBarTask $934D twice a frame (game 2893) and by $8539, which erases the bar and zeroes the power at each new aim.
$9368 PowerBarLength
$9369 PowerBarLine
$9371 c DrawCursor ; XOR the aiming cursor at ($86BA x, $86BB y): a cross of four 2-pixel arms, the centre left clear
> Pixels: (x+1, x+2 at y), (x-2, x-1 at y), (x at y-2, y-1, y+1, y+2); the centre (x, y) is never set, so the point aimed at stays visible. Drawn twice it erases: the cursor tasks $85EB (draw, move, draw), $8506 (erase on the shot), $85C8 (first draw at a new aim, x 252, y 114-116 at random) and the editor ($C042, $C116, $C20B, $C414, $C502, $C532). Counts: game 388, w1 1930, f1 122, p2 43, v1 46, m1 244.
$9395 c DrawPowerBox ; Draw the power bar's box: a rectangle x 2-131, y 8-15
> The top y 8 and the bottom y 15, 130 pixels from x 2; the sides one pixel at x 2 and x 131 for y 9-14. Once a game (NewGameScreen $95DA, game 1 at frame 6185) and in the editor ($C414, v1 2).
$93A0 PowerBoxSides
$93B3 c XorLine ; XOR a horizontal run of B pixels starting at (x E, y D) onto the screen; B = 0 draws nothing
> Inputs D = y, E = x, B = length in pixels (up to 255). Keeps BC, DE; HL and A spoiled. The address: ScreenCell $95B3 of (y, x) plus y AND 7 in the high byte. The first byte's mask: B ones from bit 7 (or $FF when B >= 8), shifted right by x AND 7, the bits shifted out counted back into B; then whole bytes XOR $FF while 8 or more remain, then a last mask of the remaining B bits from the left. The run must not cross a byte column 31 boundary (INC HL).
> The busiest drawing routine: game 14976 calls, w1 57235, f1 17840, p2 12040, v1 8432, m1 28297; callers also outside this range ($A95A, $A990, $AB7B hall of fame, $B047, $B101, $B89C menu, $BC11 attract, $C1BE editor).
$93C8 XorLineShortMask ; B < 8: C = B ones from the left
$93CF XorLineFullMask ; B >= 8: C = $FF, B = the pixels after it
$93D2 XorLineShift
$93D7 XorLineShiftLoop ; shift the mask right by x AND 7, counting the lost bits back into B
$93DC XorLineShiftNext
$93DF XorLineFirst ; XOR the first byte
$93E3 XorLineBytes ; whole bytes
$93F3 XorLineLast ; the last partial byte
$93F5 XorLineLastMask
$93FD XorLineDone

@module balldraw ; The ball sprites: the draw, the 3 x 16 XOR, the screen address, the cue ball's respot, clearing for a turn
@about Every ball is a 15 x 15 sprite pre-shifted 8 times in BallSprites $D000: 56 sprites of 48 bytes (3 bytes x 16 lines, the 16th line empty), index = shift x 7 + ball, shift = x AND 7, ball 0 the cue ball, 1-6 the numbered balls. DrawBall $9548 XORs one at the screen position in SpritePos $95D8 (x, y of the sprite's top left, the ball's centre minus 7); drawing it again at the same place erases it, so every move is an erase at the old +10/+11 and a draw at the new.
$93FF c RespotCueBall ; Put the cue ball back at (40, 115), or right of it until no ball is within 16 pixels, and draw it
> Called by the end-of-shot task $8357 when the cue ball was potted, by NextTurn $97CD ($981F) and by the editor $C042. f1 ran it once (frame 1173, after the cue ball went down). Zeroes the cue ball's velocity and friction steps (+4..+9), flags +13 = $80 (on the table), x = 40 / y = 115 with zero fractions. For each object ball on the table with |dx| < 16 and |dy| < 16 it moves x one pixel right and starts again, up to x 200. Then SpritePos = (x-7, y-7) (also +10/+11) and DrawBall ball 0.
$941E RespotTry
$9445 RespotCheckBall
$9455 RespotDy
$9461 RespotNear
$946C RespotNextBall
$947D c ClearTableForTurn ; Before the turn passes: XOR off every ball outside the middle of the table, then clear the middle (x 16-239, y 64-167)
> Called by TurnSwap $9C15 (p2 once, frame 1778). Ball 0..6: a ball whose sprite lies inside x 16-223, y 64-150 (+10, +11) is left (the block clear wipes it); every other ball is XOR-erased at its +10/+11: the balls near the cushions and the potted row (bit 7 of +13 clear). The cue ball is skipped when it is off the table (it has no image then). Then 13 character rows x 28 bytes from (y 64, x 16) are zeroed (pixels only; the attributes stay).
$9489 ClearTurnBall
$94A7 ClearTurnErase
$94BA ClearTurnNext
$94C5 ; y 64, x 16: a y/x pair for ScreenCell, not a screen address
$94CA ClearTurnRow
$94D1 ClearTurnLine
$94EB c ClearMessageRows ; Clear character rows 2-4 (y 16-39): pixels zero, attributes $39
> The message area under the HUD: the foul texts and the score tally print at row 3, and the potted row (balls at y 20-34) lies here too, so the caller redraws it after (DrawPottedRow $9AB5). Called by MessagesTask $9CB7 (steps 1 and $4E) and ScoreTally $98FF: game 8, w1 43, f1 9, p2 6, v1 15.
$94F0 ClearMessageRow
$9506 ClearMessageLine
$9520 c DrawAllBalls ; XOR every numbered ball at its +10/+11 and the cue ball when it is on the table
> Balls 1-6 are drawn whatever their flags (on the table, or in the potted row); the cue ball only with bit 7 of +13 set. Called at a new game ($8013), by NextTurn $97CD and the editor ($C042, $C532): game 1 at frame 6190, p2 2 (690, 1780).
$952B DrawAllBallsLoop
$953D DrawAllBallsNext
$9548 c DrawBall ; XOR ball A's sprite (0 cue, 1-6) at SpritePos $95D8 (x, y of the sprite's top left)
> Input A = the ball 0-6, SpritePos = (x, y). Source = BallSprites $D000 + 48 x (7 x (x AND 7) + A): BC = 48A, HL = 336 (x AND 7), the sum plus $D000 into DE. Target: ScreenCell $95B3 of (y, x) plus y AND 7 lines. A screen character row holds 8 lines, so the 16 lines go in three parts through XorSpriteLines $959F: 8 - (y AND 7) lines, then 8 lines from the next character row (y + 8), then (y AND 7) - 1 lines from the row after (y + 16): 16 lines when y AND 7 = 0, else 15; the 16th line of every sprite is zero, so nothing is lost and a second draw always erases exactly.
> 1827 calls in game, w1 15290, f1 4407, p2 10210, v1 7119, m1 3193; callers: the ball redraw task $8297, $8357, RespotCueBall $93FF, ClearTableForTurn $947D, DrawAllBalls $9520, LoseLife $9754, EraseBallsUnderBanner $98B5, DrawPottedRow $9AB5, DrawLives $9AD9, Rack $9F6F, the editor $C20B.
$959F c XorSpriteLines ; XOR B lines of 3 bytes from DE onto the screen at HL (INC H a line: within one character row)
> DE advances 3 bytes a line (the sprite is stored line by line, 3 bytes each); L goes +1, +1, -2, H + 1. 27602 calls in game (5230 DrawBall calls), w1 230986.
$95B3 c ScreenCell ; HL = the display address of the character cell holding pixel (x L, y H): line 0 of that cell
> H = $40 + 8 x (y / 64), L = 32 x ((y / 8) AND 7) + x / 8. The caller adds y AND 7 to H for the exact line. Used by XorLine, DrawBall, ClearTableForTurn, ClearMessageRows and the hall of fame ($A9CC, $AA74, $AABD, $AB7B, $ABC3) and $B118: game 9793 calls.
$95CD c AttrFromScreen ; HL = the attribute address of the display cell address HL (line 0 of the cell)
> H = $58 + (H - $40) / 8. Used by ClearMessageRows $94EB only (game 24 calls).
$95D8 b SpritePos 2 ; The sprite's screen position for DrawBall $9548: +0 x, +1 y of the top left (the ball's centre minus 7)
> Written before every DrawBall (from a record's +10/+11, from $9F36, or computed for the potted row and the lives); read by DrawBall at $9555 / $956A / $957F / $958E and by DrawLives $9AFA.

@module hud ; The new game's screen, the HUD texts, the 1up / 2up blink, the "last shot" blink
@about Text records for PrintText $9E65: row, column, attribute (bit 7 = double width), length, then the characters (no end marker). Lower case and digits come from the game's font $DEF4, everything else from the ROM's character set (printing module).
@about The HUD, rows 0 and 1, attribute $38 for the words and $3A for the numbers: "1up" col 0 and the player 1 score cols 3-8, "hi" col 12 and the hi score cols 14-19, "2up" col 23 and the player 2 score cols 26-31; row 1: the power box (cols 0-16), "frame" col 17 and the frame number cols 22-23, "shots" col 26 and the shots left col 31. Scores print as five digits plus a fixed "0" (the score x 10).
$95DA c NewGameScreen ; Start a game: clear the screen, colours, HUD, scores, frames, lives, the table, the power box; park all balls
> Called once by the game start $8013 (game frame 6182, w1 622, v1 twice: 772, 2603). Clears the display, border 7 (white; BORDCR $38), attributes: rows 0-4 $39 (the HUD and message rows), row 5 $38 (the top pocket marks), rows 6-22 $60 (the table: bright green paper, black ink), row 23 $38 (the bottom pocket marks).
> Prints the HUD words (HudTexts $969D "1up" ... HudShots), zeroes the scores ($9F45, $9F47) and the pocket turns (PlayerVars $9F43 / $9F44), frames $9F4B / $9F4C = 1, lives $9F4E / $9F4F = 3, Player2Racked $9B33 = 0; prints the three scores (PrintHudNumber 0, 1, 2), the power box, power $86BF / $86C0 = 0, the table; ParkBalls $967B for both ball sets ($8A88 and $8AEA).
$967B c ParkBalls ; Take the set at IX off the table: cue ball and balls 1-6 bit 7 of +13 clear, balls 1-6 to the potted row (x 0, 24, ... 120; y 20)
> Input IX = a set of 7 records (14 bytes each). Only +10/+11 (the sprite's place) and bit 7 of +13 are set; nothing is drawn. Called twice by NewGameScreen (game 2, frame 6190): $8A88 the current player's set, $8AEA the other player's (the set TurnSwap $9C15 exchanges).
$9684 ; y 20, x 0: the potted row's first place
$968A ParkBallsLoop
$969D b HudTexts 16 ; The HUD's text records (PrintText format: row, column, attribute, length, text)
> All read: NewGameScreen prints $969D, $96AB, $96B9, $96BF, $96D4, $96DD; UpBlinkTask $96E6 alternates $969D / $96A4 and $96AB / $96B2; ShotsBlinkTask $970E reprints $96DD.
$969D HudTexts ; row 0, col 0, attr $38, 3: "1up"
$96A1 ; "1up"
$96A4 HudOneUpBlank ; row 0, col 0, attr $39, 3: "   "
$96A8 ; "   "
$96AB HudTwoUp ; row 0, col 23, attr $38, 3: "2up"
$96AF ; "2up"
$96B2 HudTwoUpBlank ; row 0, col 23, attr $39, 3: "   "
$96B6 ; "   "
$96B9 HudHi ; row 0, col 12, attr $38, 2: "hi"
$96BD ; "hi"
$96BF HudRowOneBlank ; 17 spaces at (1, 0), attr $39: the power box
$96C3 ; 17 spaces
$96D4 HudFrame ; row 1, col 17, attr $38, 5: "frame"
$96D8 ; "frame"
$96DD HudShots ; row 1, col 26, attr $38, 5: "shots"
$96E1 ; "shots"
@res text/HudTexts.bin
$96E6 c UpBlinkTask ; Blink the current player's "1up" / "2up": print it or its blank by UpBlink $9B31, then flip UpBlink
> A task added by StartAim $8425 with period 20 (every 21 interrupts) and removed by $8483 when the shot starts, which first prints it with UpBlink = $FF (shown). CurrentPlayer $9B30 = 1 picks HudTexts ("1up", $969D) / HudOneUpBlank, 2 HudTwoUp / HudTwoUpBlank; UpBlink 0 prints the blank. game 88 runs, w1 377, p2 130.
$96F8 UpBlinkPrint1
$96FA UpBlinkPlayer2
$9703 UpBlinkPrint
$970E c ShotsBlinkTask ; With one shot left: alternate "last  " / "  shot" in place of "shots"; at the shot's end (StopShotTasks, LastShotBlink = $FF) put "shots" and the count back
> A task added by StartAim $8425 with period 25, removed by $8483, which calls it once with LastShotBlink $9B32 = $FF. Returns at once unless ShotsLeft $9F4D = 1. LastShotBlink 0 prints HudLast $9740, 1 HudShot $974A, then flips 0 / 1; $FF prints HudShots and the count (PrintHudNumber 3) and sets 0. game 72 runs, w1 308; the $FF branch only in f1 (once).
$9723 ShotsBlinkPrint
$9730 ShotsBlinkRestore
$9740 b HudLastShot 10 ; The two halves of the "last shot" blink (PrintText records at row 1, col 26, attr $38, 6 characters)
> Printed by ShotsBlinkTask $970E; both read in game, w1, f1.
$9740 HudLast ; row 1, col 26, attr $38, 6: "last  "
$9744 ; "last  "
$974A HudShot ; row 1, col 26, attr $38, 6: "  shot"
$974E ; "  shot"
@res text/HudLastShot.bin

@module gameover ; A life lost, the foul jingle, GAME OVER, the hi score and the hall of fame hand-over
$9754 c LoseLife ; A foul or no ball potted with the last shot: the foul jingle, one life less, erase one life ball; at none left GAME OVER
> Called by the end-of-shot task $8357 when the foul code $8E80 is set or the cue ball is off the table (game 4 at frames 6543-8811, w1 4, f1 4, p2 1, v1 3). With InfiniteLives $B8C4 <> 0 (the four-key cheat, flipped at $B3A1 in WaitNoKeyHeld) it goes straight to NextTurn $97CD: no jingle, no life lost. No recording of the case set it; the experiment f1c (f1 with CAPS SHIFT + SPACE + 1 + 0 held at frame 500, docs/10-gameplay-logic.md 10.10) flips it at frame 510 and keeps 3 lives through two fouls.
> Lives $9F4E - 1; negative: GameOver $97A2. Else the leftmost life ball is XORed off: a cue ball sprite at x = $E8 - 24 n (n = the lives before the loss: 3 -> x 160), y 20; then NextTurn.
$9780 c FoulJingle ; Play the foul jingle with the tasks stopped (WaitFrame $80DE with FoulJingleNotes)
> LoseLife $9754 only (game 4, w1 4, f1 4, p2 1 at frame 1668, v1 3).
$9786 c FoulJingleNotes ; Wait 10 frames, BEEPER HL $209E DE 26, wait 5 frames, BEEPER HL $29FE DE 23
> Two low notes: the ROM's BEEPER pitch 437500 / (HL + 30.125): about 52 Hz for DE 26, 27 cycles (0.51 s), then about 41 Hz for DE 23, 24 cycles (0.58 s). Run by FoulJingle through $80DE (game 4).
$9788 JingleWaitOne
$9796 JingleWaitTwo
$97A2 c GameOver ; No life left: erase the balls under the banner, print GAME OVER (and PLAYER n), then the other player's turn or the end
> From LoseLife $9754 (game 1 at frame 8811, w1 11768, f1 4445). EraseBallsUnderBanner $98B5, GameOverText at row 9. One player: GameOverEnd $9825. Two players: "PLAYER 1" / "PLAYER 2" at row 11 by CurrentPlayer; the other player's lives ($9F4F) negative too: GameOverEnd; else wait 125 frames (WaitFramesB $988C through $9886) and NextTurn $97CD (known only from the code: p2 never ran out of lives).
$97BD GameOverPlayer
$97CD c NextTurn ; After a life lost: the turn swap; with two players, rack or go on for the new player
> From LoseLife and GameOver (game 3, w1 3, f1 3, p2 1 at frame 1738, v1 3). TurnSwapStart $9C0F; one player: RET (back to $8357, which racks or aims). Two players: drops the return; when the player now at the table (the other one after a swap, the same one when TurnSwap found the other out of lives, $9C2B) has no object ball on the table (his set never racked): DrawAllBalls $9520 (XOR: erases the parked row), DrawLives $9AD9, NextFrame $9BB8 if Player2Racked $9B33 is set, Player2Racked = 1, Rack $9F69, StartAim $8425 (p2 frame 1780).
> Else (NextTurnResume $9801, known only from the code): wait 100 frames, blank row 9 (FrameBannerBlank), DrawAllBalls, DrawLives, RespotCueBall if the cue ball is off, StartAim.
$97DF NextTurnCheckBalls
$9801 NextTurnResume ; not executed in any build
$9825 GameOverEnd ; the hi score = the larger of it and both scores, printed; then the hall of fame
$9837 GameOverScore2
$9849 GameOverScore ; PrintHudNumber 2, then GameOverWait through $80DE
$9854 GameOverRestart ; back to the menu ($80B0)
$9857 c GameOverWait ; Wait 250 frames or a key, the hall of fame $A702, wait 500 frames or a key
> Run through WaitFrame $80DE by GameOverEnd (game 1 at frame 8811-9061, w1 11768-14232, f1 4445-5195). The second wait is the tail call into WaitFramesOrKey from $9860.
$9860 GameOverWaitAfter ; BC = 500 frames
$9865 c WaitFramesOrKey ; Wait up to BC frames; stop early on fire ($827C bit 4 after $8106) or any key (KEY_SCAN, K_TEST)
> game 279 frames over 2 calls (8811-9090), w1 750, f1 750.
$9884 WaitFramesOrKeyDone
$9886 c WaitFramesBStopped ; Wait B frames with the tasks stopped: WaitFrame $80DE with WaitFramesB
> From GameOver $97CA (B 125) and NextTurnResume $9801 (B 100): not executed in any build.
$988C c WaitFramesB ; HALT B times
> Code inside the text block (bytes $76 $10 $FD $C9). Not executed in any build (no two-player game over with the other player alive, no turn change to a racked set).
$9890 b GameOverTexts 16 ; GAME OVER and the losing player (PrintText records, double width, attr $60)
> GameOverText read by game, w1, f1; the two player lines are not read in any build (the recordings' games over were one-player or ended by BREAK).
$9890 GameOverText ; row 9, col 7, attr $E0, 9: "GAME OVER"
$9894 ; "GAME OVER"
$989D GameOverPlayer1 ; row 11, col 8, attr $E0, 8: "PLAYER 1"
$98A1 ; "PLAYER 1"
$98A9 GameOverPlayer2 ; row 11, col 8, attr $E0, 8: "PLAYER 2"
$98AD ; "PLAYER 2"
@res text/GameOverTexts.bin
$98B5 c EraseBallsUnderBanner ; Erase the balls on the table that the GAME OVER banner covers and take them off the table
> GameOver $97A2 (game, w1, f1 once each). A ball with bit 7 of +13 set and its sprite at x 41-199, y 57 up to 88 (one player) or 104 (two) is XORed off and its bit 7 cleared. The erase branch ran in no build (no ball sat there at the game overs).
$98BA EraseBannerLoop
$98ED EraseBannerNext

@module tally ; The score of a shot: frame x ball x pocket multiplier x 10, printed term by term and counted up into the score
@about Score of one potted ball = frame number x ball number x the pocket's multiplier (PocketMultiplierCells), shown as "FxBxMx10" and stored without the x 10; a shot's terms are added and counted into the player's score with a rising beep (v1: "1x5x3x10=150").
$98F9 c ScoreShot ; Run ScoreTally with the tasks stopped (WaitFrame $80DE)
> From the end-of-shot task $8357 twice per shot end (game 4, w1 21, f1 9, p2 6, v1 4, m1 1).
$98FF c ScoreTally ; Score the balls potted in the last shot (+12 <> 0): print each term, the total, count it into the score, refill the shots
> ShotScore $9B2C = 0. Ball 1-6 with a pocket number in +12: the first sets PotBall $9B2F and PotMultiplier $9B2E (the pocket turn (PlayerVars $9F43) + pocket, 7..12 minus 6), waits 15 frames and clears the message rows (unless MessageStep is $4F); every further one first prints the pending term with "+" (TallyTermPlus $9A01), waits, clears, takes its own ball and multiplier. The last term is printed with "=" and the total (TallyTermTotal $9A1A).
> The count: TallyPitch $9B34 = 1200, step DE = round(ShotScore / 128) + 1 (the high byte of 2 x ShotScore, plus bit 6 as the carry, plus 1); one step a frame moves DE from ShotScore into the player's score (AddScoreStep $99C1, printed with a beep) while at least DE is left (at most 127 steps; B = 45 is only counted down, never tested in the loop), then the remainder in one go (a beep even for 0), then the rest of the 45 frames waited if any; past 45 steps (ShotScore 46 or more, in no recording) JP M skips the wait (w1's longest: 36 steps and the remainder, 37 beeps, frames 8456-8493). Then ClearMessageRows, DrawPottedRow $9AB5, ShotsLeft = ShotsForFrame $9BE6, wait 5 frames.
> Returns at once when nothing was potted. Witness: AddScoreStep w1 287 calls (frames 1329-10175), p2 38, v1 72; TallyTermPlus (a double pot) w1, f1, v1 once each.
$990B TallyFindFirst
$991E TallyFirstBall
$992F TallyFirstMultiplier
$993F TallyFindNext
$994B TallyNextBall
$9956 TallyMoreBalls
$9970 TallyMoreMultiplier
$9975 TallyCount
$998C TallyCountStep
$999E TallyCountLast
$99AC TallyWaitRest
$99AF TallyDone
$99BD TallyEndWait
$99C1 c AddScoreStep ; Add DE to the current player's score, print it, beep at TallyPitch and lower the pitch by 10
> CurrentPlayer 1: ScorePlayer1 $9F45, 2: ScorePlayer2 $9F47; PrintHudNumber 0 / 1. The beep: BEEPER with HL = TallyPitch, DE = 6000 / TallyPitch (1200: about 356 Hz, DE 5, so 6 cycles, 15.5 ms), a tick that rises in pitch step by step; TallyPitch - 10 unless it would go below zero. w1 287 runs.
> DE = 6000 / TallyPitch (by subtraction) and BEEPER plays DE + 1 cycles, so every tick lasts 13.1-15.5 ms whatever its pitch. A tick after the frame's HALT ends before the next interrupt; the first tick of a count-up of 100 points or more comes straight after the printed total (T 19 296-19 781) and runs past the next interrupt, which is lost (w1 12 of 17 count-ups, f1 1 of 1; docs 9.4).
$99D0 AddScorePlayer2
$99D7 AddScorePrint
$99E6 AddScoreCycles
$99FE AddScoreDone
$9A01 c TallyTermPlus ; Add the pending term to ShotScore and print "FxBxMx10+" at row 3 (col 6, 7 for a one-digit frame)
> Double-width text, attr $38 (C = $B8). w1, f1, v1 once each (the double pots).
$9A0F TallyTermPlusPrint
$9A1A c TallyTermTotal ; Add the last term and print "FxBxMx10=" and ShotScore x 10, centred on row 3
> The column starts at 1, plus one each for a frame < 10 and a total < 1000, < 100, < 10. Each term waits 15 frames (TallyPause $9B07) before its "x". w1 17, f1 1, p2 2, v1 4.
$9A28 TallyCentre1000
$9A34 TallyCentre100
$9A3E TallyCentre10
$9A48 TallyTotalPrint
$9A67 c AddTerm ; ShotScore += frame x PotBall x PotMultiplier (ScoreTerm $9B2A as the product)
$9A87 c PrintTerm ; Print frame, "x", ball, "x", multiplier, "x", "10" at DE (double width, attr $38)
$9AA2 c PrintTermFactor ; Print HL as a number at DE, wait 15 frames, then "x"; DE past it
$9AB5 c DrawPottedRow ; XOR the potted balls (bit 7 of +13 clear) at their place in the potted row, then the lives
> After ClearMessageRows wiped rows 2-4: ScoreTally and MessagesTask step $4F. game 4, w1 21, f1 4, p2 3, v1 7. Falls into DrawLives.
$9ABB PottedRowLoop
$9AD4 PottedRowNext
$9AD9 c DrawLives ; XOR one cue ball per life (LivesLeft $9F4E, 1-3) in the potted row at x 160, 184, 208 (right-aligned to 208), y 20
> The first x = $88 + 24 (4 - lives). Lives 0 or negative: nothing. Called by DrawPottedRow, NextTurn, TurnSwap and the game start $8013: game 5, w1 22, p2 6.
$9AEA LivesFirstX
$9AF4 LivesLoop
$9B07 c TallyPause ; Wait 15 frames (HALT)
$9B09 TallyPauseLoop
$9B0D c MultiplyTerm ; ScoreTerm $9B2A = ScoreTerm x A (16 x 8 bits, shift and add)
$9B19 MultiplyLoop
$9B1D MultiplyShift
$9B2A b ScoreVars 12 ; The tally's and the turns' variables
> Readers and writers: the tally routines; CurrentPlayer by the game start $8052, TurnSwap $9C34 and the hall of fame $A79C / $A7C2 / $AD1C; UpBlink by StartAim $8432 / $8483; LastShotBlink $843E / $849F.
$9B2A ScoreTerm ; word: the product of one term (frame x ball x multiplier)
$9B2C ShotScore ; word: the shot's score (without the x 10), counted down into the player's score
$9B2E PotMultiplier ; the pending term's pocket multiplier 1-6
$9B2F PotBall ; the pending term's ball number 1-6
$9B30 CurrentPlayer ; 1 or 2
$9B31 UpBlink ; 0 = print the 1up / 2up blank next, else the word
$9B32 LastShotBlink ; 0 "last", 1 "shot", $FF restore "shots"
$9B33 Player2Racked ; 0 until the second player's set was racked once (two players)
$9B34 TallyPitch ; word: the count-up beep's BEEPER period, 1200 down by 10 a step

@module turns ; The FRAME banner, the next frame, the shots per frame, the turn swap, the last-shot beep, the messages task
$9B36 c ShowFrameBanner ; Print "FRAME n" double width at row 9 (row 11 under a PLAYER n banner), wait 75 frames, blank rows 9 and 11
> Called by Rack $9F6F at each rack (game 1 at frame 6190, w1 4, p2 2). Two players: the cell row 9, col 8 is tested for pixels (PlayerBanner printed): then row 11. The column: 9, 8 for a frame >= 10. FrameBanner $9B8E is patched (row, column), printed, the number after it (PrintNumber $9E13, attr $E0); then FrameBannerBlank $9B98 printed at row 9 and row 11 (its row byte patched).
$9B47 FrameBannerTestCell
$9B50 FrameBannerRow
$9B5E FrameBannerColumn
$9B75 FrameBannerWait
$9B8E b FrameBanner 16 ; The FRAME banner and the blank line that wipes it (PrintText records; the row and column bytes are patched)
> Written by ShowFrameBanner ($9B51, $9B5E, $9B7A, $9B85) and NextTurn ($9808); read by PrintText. In the image $9B8E = row 11, col 9.
$9B8E FrameBannerRowByte ; row (9 or 11, patched)
$9B8F ; column 9 (8 from frame 10), attr $E0, 6
$9B92 ; "FRAME "
$9B98 FrameBannerBlank ; row (patched 9 / 11), col 2, attr $60, 28 spaces
$9B9C ; 28 spaces
@res text/FrameBanner.bin
$9BB8 c NextFrame ; Frame cleared: frame + 1 (up to 99), the pocket multipliers turn one place, the shots refilled, power reset
> From the end-of-shot task $8357 and NextTurn $97CD: w1 3 times (frames 4023-10196, frames 2, 3 and 4). Frame $9F4B + 1, printed (PrintHudNumber 4); the pocket turn (PlayerVars $9F43) - 1 (0 wraps to 5), the marks redrawn (DrawPocketMarks $9D6B); ShotsLeft = ShotsForFrame, printed (PrintHudNumber 3); tail call $8539 (erase the power bar, power 0).
$9BC1 NextFrameStore
$9BD2 NextFrameTurn
$9BE6 c ShotsForFrame ; A = the shots for this frame: ShotsPerFrame[frame], 2 from frame 10; plus variation + 1 in a variation
> Frame $9F4B; the variation $B8C3 (0 standard, 1 numeric order, 2 own pockets; the menu). Standard: frames 1-2 five shots, 3-5 four, 6-9 three, then two. Variation 2 on frame 1: 5 + 3 = 8 (v1, WALKTHROUGH 6.0). game 4, w1 24.
$9BF1 ShotsFromTable
$9BF9 ShotsTableRead
$9BFA ShotsVariation
$9C06 b ShotsPerFrame 9 ; The shots of frames 1-9: 5, 5, 4, 4, 4, 3, 3, 3, 3
> Read by ShotsForFrame $9BE6 (index frame - 1). Frames 1-4 read (w1 reached frame 4); $9C0A-$9C0E (frames 5-9) read by no build.
@res tables/ShotsPerFrame.bin
$9C0F c TurnSwapStart ; Run TurnSwap with the tasks stopped (WaitFrame $80DE)
$9C15 c TurnSwap ; After a life lost: with two players and the other still alive, swap everything to the other player; then the frame, shots, pocket marks, PLAYER n
> Run by TurnSwapStart (game 3, w1 3, f1 3, p2 1 at frame 1738, v1 3). Two players (p2 only): wait 40 frames, ClearTableForTurn $947D, DrawLives (XOR: erases the lives), then unless the other's lives $9F4F are negative: CurrentPlayer 1 <-> 2, and the pairs pocket turn (PlayerVars $9F43) / $9F44, LivesLeft $9F4E / $9F4F, FrameNumber $9F4B / $9F4C swapped, the 98-byte ball sets $8A88 / $8AEA exchanged byte by byte.
> Then for one and two players: $8539 (power 0), PrintHudNumber 4 (frame), ShotsLeft = ShotsForFrame, PrintHudNumber 3, DrawPocketMarks, and falls into PlayerBanner.
$9C1D TurnSwapWait
$9C5B TurnSwapBalls ; exchange 98 bytes: $8A88-$8AE9 with $8AEA-$8B4B
$9C65 TurnSwapHud
$9C7B c PlayerBanner ; Two players: print "PLAYER 1" / "PLAYER 2" double width at row 9, col 8
> From TurnSwap and the game start $8013. Returns at once with one player ($B8BB = 1). p2 2, v1 5 (entries).
$9C8C PlayerBannerPrint
$9C8F b PlayerBannerTexts 12 ; PLAYER 1 / PLAYER 2 (PrintText records)
> Read by PlayerBanner (p2). Wiped by ShowFrameBanner's blank of row 9.
$9C8F PlayerBanner1 ; row 9, col 8, attr $E0, 8: "PLAYER 1"
$9C93 ; "PLAYER 1"
$9C9B PlayerBanner2 ; row 9, col 8, attr $E0, 8: "PLAYER 2"
$9C9F ; "PLAYER 2"
@res text/PlayerBannerTexts.bin
$9CA7 c LastShotBeepTask ; With one shot left: a short beep (BEEPER HL 500, DE 10, so 11 cycles: 825 Hz, 12.7 ms; f1 measured 825.7 Hz, 13 beeps, docs/09-sound.md 9.4), then DI (it runs in the interrupt)
> A task added by StartAim $8425 with period 25, removed by $8506 at the shot. 58 runs in game, w1 179 (none beeped: no aim of w1 had one shot left), f1 13 beeps. BEEPER ends with EI; the DI keeps the interrupt handler closed.
$9CB7 c MessagesTask ; Step through the foul message: clear, print, hold about 75 steps, clear, redraw the potted row
> MessageStep $9F50: 0: start (step 1) only when the foul code $8E80 is set; 1: ClearMessageRows; 2: the text by $8E80 (1 FOUL SHOT!, 2 WRONG ORDER!, 3 WRONG POCKET!); 3-$4D: wait; $4E: ClearMessageRows; $4F: DrawPottedRow; $50: stays. Each step but the last adds 1 (MessageNextStep pushed as the return address). A task of the balls-moving list $84BA (period 0) and called by $8457 once a frame after the balls stop until step $50. game 606 runs (320 steps); the texts: game 4, w1 4, f1 3, p2 1, v1 3 (WRONG ORDER / WRONG POCKET in v1 at frames 1471 / 3100).
$9CDE MessagePrint
$9CE1 MessageLaterSteps
$9CF0 MessageIdle
$9CF5 MessageNextStep ; MessageStep + 1
$9CFD b FoulTexts 16 ; The three foul messages (PrintText records, double width, attr $38, row 3, centred)
> Read by MessagesTask $9CB7 step 2.
$9CFD FoulShotText ; row 3, col 6, attr $B8, 10: "FOUL SHOT!"
$9D01 ; "FOUL SHOT!"
$9D0B WrongOrderText ; row 3, col 4, attr $B8, 12: "WRONG ORDER!"
$9D0F ; "WRONG ORDER!"
$9D1B WrongPocketText ; row 3, col 3, attr $B8, 13: "WRONG POCKET!"
$9D1F ; "WRONG POCKET!"
@res text/FoulTexts.bin
$9D2C c ResetBallPositions ; Put the seven balls of the current set at their rack positions (StartPositions), on the table
> x / y whole parts from StartPositions, fractions 0, +10/+11 = x - 7 / y - 7, flags +13 = $80; velocities untouched; nothing drawn. Called by the entry $8000 (every build, frame 0 / 5556) and the editor ($C042, $C532).
$9D38 ResetBallLoop
$9D5D b StartPositions 2 ; The rack: x, y of the cue ball and balls 1-6
> (40, 115), (190, 115), (205, 106), (205, 124), (221, 98), (221, 115), (221, 132): a triangle of six with its point at ball 1. Read by ResetBallPositions; the rack scripts end on the same points (RackScripts0).
@res tables/StartPositions.bin

@module printing ; The pocket multiplier marks, the number printer, the text printer and its glyph lookup
@about PrintChar $9E7C prints character A at row H, column L with attribute C. C bit 7 = double width: the attribute (C AND $7F) goes into two cells and the glyph is widened (PrintCharWide); the string and number printers then step two columns. Glyphs: $61-$7A (a-z) Font $DEF4, $30-$39 (0-9) Font+208, $7F the copyright sign CopyrightGlyph $9F2E, every other code CHAR_SET + 8 (code - $20) (upper case, space, punctuation from the ROM).
$9D6B c DrawPocketMarks ; Print "xN" at the six pockets, clockwise from the top left, N = 1-6 turned by the pocket turn (PlayerVars $9F43)
> Cells: top row 5 (y 40) cols 0, 15, 30 then bottom row 23 (y 184) cols 30, 15, 0, each a ROM "x" and a digit. The k-th mark (k = 0..5) shows ((PocketTurn + k) mod 6) + 1; PocketTurn ends where it started (six increments). The scoring and the own-pocket rule use PocketTurn + the pocket number the same way ($9923, $8C37), so pocket 1 is the top left mark (known only from the code). game 4 (frame 6190 on), w1 7, v1 5.
$9D8C c PrintPocketMark ; At screen address HL: the ROM's "x" glyph, then the digit PocketTurn + 1; PocketTurn + 1 mod 6
$9D8C ; CHAR_SET+704: the ROM's "x"
$9DA4 PocketMarkDigit
$9DB0 PocketMarkStore
$9DB4 c CopyGlyph ; Copy 8 bytes from DE down the cell at HL; in the bottom two thirds one byte earlier (the glyph one pixel lower)
$9DBA CopyGlyphStart
$9DBC CopyGlyphLoop
$9DC3 c PrintHudNumber ; Print HUD number A: 0 score 1, 1 score 2, 2 hi score (five digits and a "0"), 3 shots left, 4 frame (and a space)
> Positions (row, column): score 1 (0, 3), score 2 (0, 26), hi (0, 14), shots (1, 31), frame (1, 22); attr $3A. The scores print with leading zeros (B = $80), shots and frame without. game 15, w1 322, v1 92.
$9DD5 HudNumberScore2
$9DE3 HudNumberHi
$9DEF HudNumberTimesTen ; the fixed "0" after a score
$9DF5 HudNumberSmall
$9E13 c PrintNumber ; Print HL (0-65535) in decimal at row D, column E, attr C; B bit 7 = keep leading zeros. DE out: past the number
> Five digits by subtraction (10000, 1000, 100, 10, 1, PrintDigit $9E40); without B bit 7 the zeros before the first other digit are skipped and 0 prints as "0". Callers: the HUD, the tally, the FRAME banner, the hall of fame $AA23. game 16, w1 420.
$9E20 PrintNumberDigits
$9E38 PrintNumberUnits
$9E40 c PrintDigit ; One digit: A = HL / DE (repeated subtraction), print it at the position kept on the stack, step the column
> Swaps its return address and the position kept on the stack by the caller (EX (SP),HL) so the five calls share the position.
$9E41 PrintDigitCount
$9E49 PrintDigitShow
$9E54 PrintDigitPrint
$9E61 PrintDigitDone
$9E65 c PrintText ; Print the text record at HL: row, column, attribute (bit 7 double width), length N, then N characters
> No end marker: the length byte counts. Out: HL = row (H), column (L) after the text (callers print a number there); DE past the record. game 138 calls, w1 449; callers in the HUD, the tally, the hall of fame ($AA4B, $ACEF), the menu ($B22E, $B3F3, $B605, $B67E), the presents page $B8C5 and the editor ($C042, $C414).
$9E6E PrintTextLoop
$9E78 PrintTextNext
$9E7C c PrintChar ; Print character A at row H (0-23), column L (0-31), attribute C (bit 7: double width); keeps all registers
> Writes the attribute first (one or two cells), then the glyph (see the module text). game 1094 calls, w1 4388, m1 2143.
$9E92 PrintCharCopyright
$9E9D PrintCharDigit
$9EAC PrintCharLower
$9EBB PrintCharRom ; any other code: CHAR_SET + 8 (A - $20)
$9EC0 PrintCharGlyph
$9ED2 PrintCharNarrow
$9ED4 PrintCharNarrowLoop
$9EDA PrintCharDone
$9EDE c PrintCharWide ; Copy glyph DE to cells HL and HL+1 with every pixel doubled across (16 pixels a line)
> Each glyph byte's low four bits go, doubled, to the right byte, the high four to the left. game 56, w1 285.
$9EE0 PrintCharWideLoop
$9F14 c CellAddress ; HL = the display address of row H (0-23), column L (0-31), line 0
$9F23 c CellAttr ; HL = the attribute address of the display cell address HL
$9F2E b CopyrightGlyph 8 ; The glyph of character $7F, the copyright sign (a C in a ring)
> Read by PrintChar's $7F branch ($9E96: game 2, m1 4; the menu's copyright line).
@res fonts/CopyrightGlyph.bin

@module racking ; The player variables, the rack scripts and their interpreter
$9F36 c SpriteFromBall ; HL = the sprite place of record IX: L = x - 7, H = y - 7 (whole parts +1, +3)
> Called by the ball redraw task $8297 and Rack $9F6F: game 905, w1 7580.
$9F43 b PlayerVars 8 ; The players' scores, frames, shots, lives, the message step and the six rack script states
> The pairs "current, other" ($9F43 / $9F44, $9F4B / $9F4C, $9F4E / $9F4F) are swapped by TurnSwap $9C15; the scores stay by player. Set by NewGameScreen $95DA; the hi score is 300 (shown 3000) in the image, kept across games.
$9F43 PocketTurn ; the current player's pocket mark turn 0-5 (NextFrame - 1, DrawPocketMarks)
$9F44 PocketTurnOther ; the other player's
$9F45 ScorePlayer1 ; word, score / 10
$9F47 ScorePlayer2 ; word
$9F49 HiScore ; word, 300 in the image
$9F4B FrameNumber ; the current player's frame 1-99
$9F4C FrameOther ; the other player's frame
$9F4D ShotsLeft ; set by ShotsForFrame; lowered by the end-of-shot task $83EA
$9F4E LivesLeft ; 3 at the start; negative = out
$9F4F LivesOther ; the other player's lives
$9F50 MessageStep ; MessagesTask step 0-$50 (zeroed at each shot by $84CE)
$9F51 RackScriptState ; 6 x 4 bytes, balls 1-6: script pointer (word), wait count, move count
$9F55 ; ball 2
$9F59 ; ball 3
$9F5D ; ball 4
$9F61 ; ball 5
$9F65 ; ball 6
$9F69 c Rack ; Rack the balls with the tasks stopped (WaitFrame $80DE with RackBalls)
> From the game start $8013, the end-of-shot task $8357 and NextTurn $97CD: game 1 (frame 6190), w1 4 (frames 630-10196), p2 2.
$9F6F c RackBalls ; FRAME banner, then roll balls 1-6 into the triangle by their scripts, one step a frame; then the cue ball to (40, 115)
> ShowFrameBanner $9B36; the rack set = RackSets[((frame - 1) / 2) mod 6] (frames 1-2 set 0, 3-4 set 1, ...); each ball's RackScriptState = (its script, 0, 0) and its record cleared (ClearBallMotion $A040).
> Each step: RackStep $A05E for balls 1-6 (B counts the scripts ended), the draw list $869E rebuilt ($8304), HALT, and every ball of the list with bit 4 of +13 set (it moved) erased at +10/+11, placed at SpriteFromBall, drawn, bit 4 cleared (the list's cue entry, ball 0, is skipped). Until all six scripts ended: game 170 steps (frames 6266-6435).
> The cue ball: ClearBallMotion, x 40 / y 115 with a random fraction (R x 2 into both), drawn (no erase before it).
$9F78 RackSetMod
$9F80 RackSetPick
$9F94 RackInitBall
$9FAE RackStepAll
$9FB9 RackStepBall
$9FD8 RackRedraw
$A00E RackRedrawNext
$A040 c ClearBallMotion ; Zero record IX's fractions (+0, +2), velocities (+4..+7), friction steps (+8, +9); flags +13 = $80
$A05E c RackStep ; One frame of the rack script of ball IX, state at HL: a wait, a repeated move, or the next command
> State: +0/+1 the script pointer, +2 the wait, +3 the moves left. A wait above 0: one less, return. Moves left: x + dx, y + dy (the pair at the pointer, whole pixels), bit 4 of +13 set; the pointer passes the pair when the count ends. Else the next command: 0 end (B + 1, the pointer stays: every later step ends again), 1 x y (put the ball at x, y), 2 n dx dy (move n times by dx, dy, one a frame; starts at once), 3 n (wait n frames).
> game 1040 calls (frames 6266-6435); command 1: 12, command 2: 20, command 3: 6.
$A068 RackStepMove
$A089 RackStepCommand
$A09B RackStepEnd ; command 0
$A09D RackStepPlace ; command 1
$A0B1 RackStepRun ; command 2
$A0BB RackStepWait ; command 3
$A0C4 w RackSets 6 ; The six rack sets: pointers to six script pointers each (balls 1-6)
> Read by RackBalls ($9F83): sets 0 and 1 read; $A0C8-$A0CF (sets 2-5, frames 5-12) read by no build. Set 1 $A162 and sets 2-5 ($A1E4, $A29C, $A38E, $A4F8) are past this fragment.
@res racks/RackScripts.bin
$A0D0 w RackScripts0 6 ; Set 0 (frames 1-2): the scripts of balls 1-6
@res racks/RackScripts.bin
$A0DC b RackScript0Ball1 16 ; Ball 1: wait 75; at (7, 175); 30 x (+2, -2); 61 x (+2, 0); at (190, 115); end
> The scripts of set 0 all start at a left corner pocket, (7, 175) or (7, 55), run diagonally to y 115, along the middle line, turn into their place and end exactly on StartPositions. Waits 75, 60, 45, 30, 15, 0: ball 6 rolls first.
@res racks/RackScripts.bin
$A0ED RackScript0Ball2 ; ball 2: set0.ball2
$A106 RackScript0Ball3 ; ball 3: set0.ball3
$A11F RackScript0Ball4 ; ball 4: set0.ball4
$A138 RackScript0Ball5 ; ball 5: set0.ball5
$A149 RackScript0Ball6 ; ball 6: set0.ball6

@module rackscripts ; The rack-in scripts of sets 1-5: how the six object balls roll onto the table before frames 3-12
@about The racking $9F6F (the lead's range) animates the six object balls into the rack before each frame. It picks a set from the table $A0C4 by the frame number $9F4B: set ((frame - 1) / 2) mod 6, so frames 1-2 use set 0 ($A0D0, the lead's range), 3-4 set 1, 5-6 set 2, 7-8 set 3, 9-10 set 4, 11-12 set 5, and from frame 13 on the sets repeat. A set is six words, the script of ball 1 to ball 6, then the scripts.
@about A script is a byte program run one step a frame by $A05E for its ball, in a 4-byte slot at $9F51 (script pointer, wait count, move count): 0 = the end (the ball is done; the racking ends when all six are), 1 x y = place the ball at pixel (x, y) (+1 / +3 of its record), 2 n dx dy = move by (dx, dy) on each of the next n frames (signed bytes, mostly 2 pixels a frame), 3 n = wait n frames. Every script ends with a place command on the ball's rack position of $9D5D ((190, 115), (205, 106), (205, 124), (221, 98), (221, 115), (221, 132)), so a step count off by one does not matter.
@about Set 1 ($A162-$A1E3) is read in w1 at frames 3 and 4. Sets 2-5, the 1310 bytes $A1E4-$A701, are read by no recording: no game reached frame 5 (w1 won frames 1-3). The only words in the image that point into $A1E4-$A701 are the four set pointers $A0C8-$A0CE and the sets' own script pointers (a byte search of work/mkgame/original.bin; other matches are instruction bytes). The scripts decode cleanly and each ends on its ball's rack position (work/stage2/ann/04-scratch/racks.py): they are the racks of frames 5-12, not leftover data. The paths (from the decoded scripts, not seen on screen): set 1 starts the balls from the four corners and the middles of the top and bottom cushions; set 2 runs them along the cushions to x 27 and then right along their rack rows; set 3 brings them from the right corners across to the left cushion and back; set 4 sends three from the top left and three from the bottom right round the cushions with pauses and short side steps; set 5 sends all six along one path, in at (8, 56), each dropping into a pocket and reappearing at another (place commands at the pocket positions (247, 175), (128, 56), (247, 56), (8, 175), (128, 175), (8, 56)).
$A162 w RackSet1 6 ; Rack-in set 1: the script of each object ball 1-6, the frames 3-4 rack (stage-1 w1 racked frames 3 and 4 with it)
> Read through the set table at $A0C4 by the racking $9F6F (index ((frame - 1) / 2) mod 6, the frame from $9F4B): w1 read every byte of this set twice (frames 3 and 4 of its game; l1sum.py --reads A162-A1E3: w1 only, 1562 reads). No other recording reached frame 3.
@res racks/RackScripts.bin
