@module halloffame ; The hall of fame: the qualifying test, the flags on their poles, the slide of the flags, the table
@about The hall of fame is three slots of 71 bytes at HofTable $AC08, fixed on screen: slot 1 (centre, the highest pole, yellow), slot 2 (left, magenta), slot 3 (right, green). A slot holds the score (a word, shown x 10), the name (7 characters), the flag's current position, the pole's position, the flag's attribute, a "moves" flag and the name rendered as a 7 x 8-byte bitmap (inverted glyphs: the flag is solid ink, the letters paper). The ranking moves the score and name only (9 bytes); the positions and colours stay with the slot.
@about At GAME OVER ($9857) GameOverHallOfFame $A702 counts the slots a score beats (CountBeaten $A7EB, ties do not qualify). If it beats none, nothing happens (game and f1). Otherwise the name page and name entry run ($ACEF, module nameentry), then the hall of fame is drawn (ShowHallOfFame $A8FD: poles, flags, scores), the flags that lose their place slide down off the screen (ShowAndSlideDown $A86D), the new entry is inserted (InsertEntry $A817) and the flags slide back up to their poles with the new names (SlideFlagsUp $A8C7). w1 ran it all at frames 12018-13732 with the name "vp" (screens w1 frames 12500, 13300, 14000). The attract sequence also shows the table through ShowHallOfFame ($B906, when $BD0B is set).
@about The two-player path ($A745-$A7E8) enters a name for each qualifying player ("congratulations! player 1 / 2", $9B30 = 1 / 2) and inserts both; no recording ran it (p2 ended by BREAK): known only from the code.
$A702 c GameOverHallOfFame ; At GAME OVER: enter the hall of fame if the score qualifies
> Called once by $985D (the GAME OVER path $9857). Clears the "moves" flag (+$0E) of the three slots. One player (Players $B8BB = 1): copies the score $9F45 to NewEntry1 $ACDD, counts the slots it beats (CountBeaten $A7EB) and returns if none; else runs the name page and entry (NameEntryPage $ACEF), copies NameBuffer $B227 to NewEntryName1 $ACDF, then ShowAndSlideDown $A86D, InsertEntry $A817 for NewEntry1 and SlideFlagsUp $A8C7 (a jump: its RET returns to $9860).
> Witness: game 1 (frame 9061) and f1 1 (frame 4695) returned at $A72A (score below every slot); w1 1 (frame 12018) went on to the name entry; $A72E w1 1, frames 13368-13553.
$A72E OnePlayerQualified ; store the name typed and show the hall of fame
$A745 TwoPlayers ; B = the slots player 1 beats, C = player 2's
$A767 TwoPlayersOneLast ; one of them beats only slot 3 and the other more: the one with 1 is dropped
$A771 TwoPlayersBothLast ; both beat only slot 3: the lower score is dropped (a tie keeps player 1)
$A780 TwoPlayersDropFirst ; player 1's score is lower: B = 0
$A781 TwoPlayersRanked
$A783 TwoPlayersDropOne ; drop the one whose count is 1
$A78B TwoPlayersDropSecond
$A78D TwoPlayersFirst ; player 1 does not qualify: NewEntry1's score word = 0
$A799 TwoPlayersNameFirst ; name entry with $9B30 = 1, the record at NewEntry1
$A7B4 TwoPlayersSecond ; player 2 does not qualify: NewEntry2's score word = 0
$A7C0 TwoPlayersNameSecond ; name entry with $9B30 = 2, the record at NewEntry2
$A7D9 TwoPlayersShow ; show, slide down, insert both records, slide up
$A7EB c CountBeaten ; A = how many hall-of-fame slots the score HL beats, flagging them
> Input HL: the score. Tests slot 3, slot 2, slot 1 in that order with TestSlot $A802; each slot whose score is below HL (strictly) gets +$0E = 1 (its flag will move) and adds 1 to A; the first slot not beaten ends the count (TestSlot drops CountBeaten's frame and returns to the caller). Output A = 0-3, Z when 0.
> Witness: game 1, f1 1 (both A = 0), w1 2 (frames 12018 and 13553, from $A702 and InsertEntry).
$A802 c TestSlot ; Beaten-slot test for CountBeaten: score HL against slot IX
> IX: the slot. If HL > the slot's score (+0/+1): A + 1, +$0E = 1, return to CountBeaten. Else POP the return address and RET to CountBeaten's caller with the count so far. Witness: game 1, w1 6, f1 1; $A811 (beaten) w1 4.
$A811 SlotBeaten
$A817 c InsertEntry ; Insert the 9-byte record at DE (score, name) into the table and render the three names again
> DE: a new-entry record (NewEntry1 $ACDD or NewEntry2 $ACE6). A zero score returns at once (a player who did not qualify). Ranks the score with CountBeaten (which flags the slots that will change), moves slot 2 to slot 3 (rank 2 or 3) and slot 1 to slot 2 (rank 3), 9 bytes each, copies the record into the freed slot, then RenderName $AADF for all three slots.
> Witness: w1 1 at frame 13553 ("vp", 270, into slot 2: james stays first, francis moves to slot 3, bill drops out; screen w1 frame 14000).
$A841 InsertCopy ; DE = the slot the rank frees
$A851 InsertStore
$A86D c ShowAndSlideDown ; Show the hall of fame, then slide the flags that lose their place down off the screen
> ShowHallOfFame $A8FD, 50 frames' pause (HALTs), BlankScore $AA4B over each flagged slot's score, then one pixel a frame SlideFlagDown $A95A on each slot until no slot has +$0E bit 0 set (each clears it when its flag has left the screen at y $C0).
> Witness: w1 1, frames 13368-13553 ($A896 129 frames).
$A872 PauseDown
$A896 SlideDownLoop
$A8C7 c SlideFlagsUp ; After the insert: slide the flagged flags up to their poles, then print their scores
> 50 frames' pause, then one pixel a frame SlideFlagUp $A990 on each slot until no flag moves. Returns to GameOverHallOfFame's caller. Witness: w1 1, frames 13553-13732 ($A8CC 129 frames).
$A8C9 PauseUp
$A8CC SlideUpLoop
$A8FD c ShowHallOfFame ; Draw the hall of fame: blue screen, three poles, the flags with their names, the scores
> Clears the pixels, attributes $0F (white on blue), border 1 (blue). Then a frame each: DrawPole $ABC3, DrawFlag $AB7B and PrintScore $AA23 for slots 1, 2, 3. Called by ShowAndSlideDown and by the attract sequence ($B906).
> Witness: w1 1, frames 13368-13374. The attract call: no recording ran $B906 (l1sum.py B906) (known only from the code).
$A95A c SlideFlagDown ; Move slot IX's flag one pixel down (if it is flagged and still on the screen)
> Returns at once unless +$0E bit 0. At y (+$0A) >= $C0 clears the flag. Else XORs the flag's top line off and a line under it on (XorLine $93B3, 48 pixels), fills the line the name leaves (RestoreFlagLine $AABD, A = 8), moves the tail (DrawFlagTail $A9CC at y and y + 1) and draws the name one line lower (DrawNameBitmap $AA74).
> Witness: w1 387 calls, 216 moves, frames 13425-13553.
$A96B SlideFlagDownMove
$A990 c SlideFlagUp ; Move slot IX's flag one pixel up until it is back at its pole
> Returns unless +$0E bit 0. When y - 2 < the pole's y (+$0C) clears the flag and prints the score (PrintScore $AA23, a jump). Else the mirror of SlideFlagDown (RestoreFlagLine with A = $0F). Witness: w1 387 calls, 216 moves, frames 13604-13732.
$A9A6 SlideFlagUpMove
$A9CC c DrawFlagTail ; XOR the flag's ragged tail (FlagTail $AB41, 29 lines) at x + 48 of slot IX's flag
> From the flag position (+9 x, +$0A y); nothing when y >= $C0. Draws the lines of the first character row, then whole rows of 8 (clipped at the screen's bottom), through XorTailLines $AA12. Witness: w1 867 calls, frames 13372-13731.
$A9F2 TailRowLoop
$AA0E TailLastRow ; the last part row, then falls into XorTailLines
$AA12 c XorTailLines ; XOR B lines of the tail (2 bytes a line, the right byte first) at HL; C counts the lines left
> DE: the tail data, HL: the screen. Called by DrawFlagTail and fallen into from $AA0E. Witness: w1 3372 entries, 21779 lines.
$AA23 c PrintScore ; Print slot IX's score x 10 above its pole: five digits with leading zeros and a "0"
> At character column x/8 (+$0B) and row y/8 - 1 (+$0C), attribute $0F: $9E13 with B = $80 (leading zeros printed), then the character "0" through $9E7C. The 003000 / 002700 of w1 frame 14000. Witness: w1 5, frames 13374-13732.
$AA4B c BlankScore ; Print six spaces over slot IX's score (before its flag slides away)
> Patches the row and column of the text record ScoreBlank $AA6A and prints it ($9E65). Witness: w1 2 (the two flagged slots), frame 13424.
$AA6A t ScoreBlank ; A text record: row, column (both patched by BlankScore), attribute $0F, 6 spaces
> Read by $9E65 from BlankScore only (w1 20 reads). The text record format: see MenuTexts $B464.
@res text/ScoreBlank.bin
$AA74 c DrawNameBitmap ; Copy slot IX's name bitmap (+$0F, 7 bytes x 8 lines) to the flag at (x, y + 8)
> Clipped at y $C0, split over two character rows when y + 8 is not a multiple of 8, through CopyNameLines $AAAF. Reached by a jump from SlideFlagDown, SlideFlagUp and DrawFlag. Witness: w1 435, frames 13372-13731.
$AA8B NameBitmapRows
$AAAF c CopyNameLines ; Copy A lines of 7 bytes from DE to the screen at HL (down one pixel line each)
> Witness: w1 1107 entries, 3480 lines (from DrawNameBitmap and RestoreFlagLine).
$AABD c RestoreFlagLine ; Copy one solid line (SolidLine $AAD8) at slot IX's flag x, line y + A
> A = 8 (sliding down: the line above the name) or $0F (sliding up: the line under it). Nothing at y + A >= $C0. Witness: w1 432, frames 13425-13731.
$AAD8 b SolidLine ; Seven $FF bytes: a solid 56-pixel line of a flag
> Read by RestoreFlagLine through CopyNameLines (w1 2688 reads).
@res graphics/SolidLine.bin
$AADF c RenderName ; Render slot IX's name (+2, 7 characters) into its bitmap (+$0F), inverted
> RenderChar $AB04 for each character, into the bitmap with a stride of 7. Called by InsertEntry (the three slots) and DrawFlag. Witness: w1 6, frames 13372-13553.
$AAE9 RenderNameSource
$AAF3 RenderNameLoop
$AAF5 RenderNameNext
$AB04 c RenderChar ; One character A as 8 inverted bytes, every 7th byte from HL
> Digits from Font+$D0 ($DFC4), a-z from Font $DEF4, anything else from CHAR_SET $3D00 (space). Same lookup as the print routine $9E7C. Witness: w1 42 (6 names x 7), $AB23 (the ROM's glyphs, spaces) 12.
$AB14 RenderCharLetter
$AB23 RenderCharRom
$AB28 RenderCharCopy
$AB33 RenderCharLoop
$AB41 b FlagTail 2 ; The flag's ragged tail: 29 lines of 2 bytes (right byte, left byte), XORed at x + 48
> Read by XorTailLines (w1 43558 reads). Lines 0-4: right $00, left $FF (the flag goes on 8 pixels); lines 5-23: left $FF, right $7F $7E $7C $78 $78 $7C $7E $7F repeating (the ragged edge, two notches and a part); lines 24-28 ($AB71-$AB7A): the curled bottom corner below the 24-line field.
@res graphics/FlagTail.bin
$AB7B c DrawFlag ; Draw slot IX's flag at its pole: attributes, the solid field, the tail, the name
> Sets the flag position to (x + 8, y + 1) of the pole, renders the name (RenderName), fills 9 attribute cells a row with +$0D from the flag's row to the bottom of the screen, XORs a 48 x 24 solid field (XorLine $93B3), the tail (DrawFlagTail) and the name (DrawNameBitmap). Witness: w1 3, frames 13372-13373.
$AB93 FlagAttrRows
$AB9C FlagAttrCells
$ABB6 FlagFieldLines
$ABC3 c DrawPole ; Draw slot IX's pole from (x, y) to the bottom of the screen
> At the pole's top line + 7: the cap ($FF $E0, 11 pixels) with attribute $0F on its two cells; then each character row down to the bottom: attribute $0F and 8 lines of $1F (a 5-pixel pole). Witness: w1 3, frame 13371.
$ABE1 PoleRows
$ABEE PoleLines
$ABFD c AttrOfScreen ; HL = the attribute address of the screen address HL (H's character row)
> Witness: w1 99, frames 13371-13373 (DrawPole, DrawFlag).
$AC08 b HofTable 8 ; The hall of fame: 3 slots of 71 bytes; slot 1 centre top (james 300), slot 2 left (francis 200), slot 3 right (bill 100)
> Read by CountBeaten (the scores: game and f1 read only slot 3's score), InsertEntry, ShowHallOfFame and the slide routines; written by InsertEntry, RenderName (the bitmaps) and the slide routines (+9/+$0A, +$0E). In the image: +0/+1 score, +2..+8 the name (padded with spaces), +9/+$0A the flag's x / y (set by DrawFlag), +$0B/+$0C the pole's x / y in pixels, +$0D the flag's attribute, +$0E bit 0 = the flag moves, +$0F..+$46 the name bitmap (56 bytes, rendered at the first draw).
> w1 wrote "vp" with 270 into slot 2 (screen w1 frame 14000: james 003000 centre, vp 002700 left, francis 002000 right).
@res text/HofTable.bin
$AC0A ; +2..+8 the name " james "
$AC11 ; +9/+$0A the flag's position (x, y)
$AC13 ; the pole (88, 23), attribute $0E, moves
$AC17 ; +$0F..+$46 the name bitmap
$AC4F HofSlot2 ; slot 2: score 200, "francis"
$AC51 ; the name
$AC58 ; the pole (0, 63), attribute $0B, moves
$AC5E ; the name bitmap
$AC96 HofSlot3 ; slot 3: score 100, " bill  "
$AC98 ; the name
$AC9F ; the pole (176, 103), attribute $0C, moves
$ACA5 ; the name bitmap
$ACDD b NewEntry1 9 ; The new entries: player 1's score and name, then player 2's (9 bytes each, the format of a slot's first 9)
> Written by GameOverHallOfFame (the score from $9F45 / $9F47, the name from NameBuffer $B227), read by InsertEntry. w1 wrote and read NewEntry1 (11 reads); NewEntry2 is read and written only on the two-player path, which no recording ran.
$ACDF NewEntryName1 ; player 1's name
$ACE6 NewEntry2 ; player 2's score
$ACE8 NewEntryName2 ; player 2's name

@module nameentry ; The name entry: the "congratulations" page, the character grid, the cursor, the 7-character name
@about NameEntryPage $ACEF clears the screen to black on yellow, prints the title ("congratulations!", or with "player 1" / "player 2" in a two-player game, by $9B30) and the eight lines of HofTextList $AD3D, then NameEntry $AE92 draws the grid CharacterGrid $B167 (a-n, o-z space ".", 0-9 "rub" "end": 3 rows of 14 cells, 16 pixels apart), the name line of seven "_" and the cursor box, and polls the input dispatcher $8106 once a frame (screen w1 frame 12500).
@about Input bits of $827C ($8106): right ($01) or down ($08) moves the cursor forward, left ($02) or up ($04) back, with auto-repeat (RepeatDelay $B226: the first step at once, then after 7, 6, 5, 4, 3 and 2 frames, then every 2 frames); fire ($10) picks the cell: a character is stored at the name cursor (the 7th cell is overwritten while the name is full), "rub" steps back and writes "_", "end" turns the "_" into spaces, centres the name in its 7 characters and returns from the page to GameOverHallOfFame. Each step clicks (BEEPER, HL $03E8 DE $14), each pick a lower click (HL $04B0, DE $16). w1 typed "vp" and "end" (frames 12025-13368).
$ACEF c NameEntryPage ; The "congratulations" page, then the name entry
> Clears the pixels, attributes $30 (black on yellow), border 6 and BORDCR $30. The title: HofTitle $AD4F when Players $B8BB = 1, else HofTitleP1 $AD63 or HofTitleP2 $AD81 by $9B30; then each text record of HofTextList $AD3D ($9E65), then a jump to NameEntry $AE92 (its RET goes back to GameOverHallOfFame).
> Witness: w1 1, frames 12018-12023 (the title HofTitle; HofTitleP1/P2 read by no recording, l1sum.py --reads AD63-AD9E).
$AD25 PrintHofTexts
$AD2B PrintHofTextLoop
$AD3D w HofTextList 9 ; The eight text records of the name page, then a zero word
> Read by NameEntryPage (w1 18 reads).
$AD4F t HofTitle ; "congratulations!" (row 0, column 8, attribute $30, 16 characters)
> The text records of the page (format: MenuTexts $B464), all attribute $30, read by NameEntryPage in w1 (the "player" titles by none).
$AD63 HofTitleP1 ; at (0, 3), 26 chars
$AD81 HofTitleP2 ; at (0, 3), 26 chars
$AD9F HofText1 ; at (2, 0), 32 chars
$ADC3 HofText2 ; at (3, 0), 30 chars
$ADE5 HofText3 ; at (4, 0), 5 chars
$ADEE HofText4 ; at (6, 0), 32 chars
$AE12 HofText5 ; at (7, 0), 31 chars
$AE35 HofText6 ; at (8, 0), 31 chars
$AE58 HofText7 ; at (9, 0), 29 chars
$AE79 HofText8 ; at (10, 0), 21 chars
@res text/HofTitle.bin
$AE92 c NameEntry ; Draw the grid and the empty name, then move the cursor and pick characters until "end"
> DrawCharacterGrid $B0C7, NameBuffer $B227 = seven "_" ($5F), the cursor at row 0 column 0 (D, E; XorCursorBox $B047), RepeatDelay $B226 = $70, HL = the name cursor = NameBuffer, DrawName $B0A8. Then the loop NameEntryLoop $AEB3, a frame each: HALT, $8106, with $AEB3 pushed as the return address of every branch. Nothing held: RepeatDelay = $70. Fire: PickCell. A direction: auto-repeat, click, NameCursorForward $AFFE or NameCursorBack $B023.
> Witness: w1 1 (frame 12023); NameEntryLoop 1230 frames, 12025-13368; 51 cursor steps ($AF00), 3 picks ($AF26: v, p, end).
$AEB3 NameEntryLoop ; HL = the name cursor, D/E = the cursor's row/column
$AECC NameEntryDirection
$AEE8 NameEntryRepeat ; RepeatDelay - 1; a step only when its low nibble wraps to $F
$AF00 NameEntryStep ; the next delay: high nibble - 1 (at least 1) in both nibbles; click; $01/$08 forward, else back
$AF26 NameEntryFire ; fire: a lower click, PickCell, then wait until no input
$AF3E NameEntryRelease
$AF4E c PickCell ; Fire on the cell at row D column E: store the character, rub out, or end
> The cell's byte is CharacterGrid[14 D + E]. A character: stored at HL (at the 7th position the last character is overwritten), HL + 1, DrawName (a jump). $0C "rub": HL - 1 (not before the start), "_" there, DrawName. $0D "end": drops the two return addresses (NameEntryFire's and $AEB3) so its RET leaves the name entry; turns "_" into spaces, counts the leading (D) and trailing (E) spaces and shifts the name by (D - E) / 2 to centre it.
> Witness: w1 3 (frames 13102-13368): two characters ($AF76 2), one "end" ($AF8C 1, shifted right: $AFDE). Rub ($AF7B) and the left shift ($AFD1) were not run.
$AF5E PickCellByte
$AF76 PickStore
$AF7B PickRub
$AF87 PickRubMark
$AF8C PickEnd
$AF93 EndBlankLoop
$AF9A EndBlankNext
$AFA4 CountLeadLoop
$AFAD CountTrail
$AFB4 CountTrailLoop
$AFBD CentreName
$AFD1 CentreLeft ; more leading spaces: copy the name left
$AFD9 CentreLeftFill
$AFDE CentreRight ; more trailing spaces: copy it right (LDDR)
$AFF1 CentreRightCopy
$AFF9 CentreRightFill
$AFFE c NameCursorForward ; Move the cursor box one cell forward (row 2: column 10 "rub" to 12 "end", 12 back to the start)
> XorCursorBox off, the next cell (column 14 wraps to the next row), XorCursorBox on (a jump). Columns 11 and 13 of row 2 are never reached (the rub and end cells are two wide). Witness: w1 45, frames 12933-13359.
$B00F ForwardFromRub
$B018 ForwardNext
$B023 c NameCursorBack ; Move the cursor box one cell back (row 2: 12 to 10; before row 0 column 0: "end")
> Witness: w1 6, frames 13114-13154.
$B034 BackPrevious
$B047 c XorCursorBox ; XOR the cursor box round the cell at row D, column E
> Cell (D, E) at pixel x = 16 E + $14, y = 16 D + $70; a box 12 pixels wide (28 for rub and end: row 2, column >= 10), its corners drawn as XorLine $93B3 lines. Drawing twice erases. Witness: w1 103, frames 12024-13359.
$B057 CursorBoxDraw
$B0A8 c DrawName ; Draw NameBuffer's 7 characters at y $B0, x $48 + 16 n
> DrawCharAt $B118 for each. Witness: w1 3, frames 12024-13163.
$B0B3 DrawNameLoop
$B0C7 c DrawCharacterGrid ; Draw the 42 cells of CharacterGrid (3 rows of 14 at x $10 + 16 n, y $70 + 16 row), then "rub" and "end"
> The $0C / $0D cells print as spaces; the words "rub" and "end" are drawn as line lists (DrawLineList $B101 with RubLines $B191 at x $B7, y $90 and EndLines $B1DA at x $D7, y $90). Witness: w1 1, frame 12023.
$B0CF GridRowLoop
$B0D3 GridCellLoop
$B101 c DrawLineList ; XOR a list of horizontal lines (dx, dy, length; $FF ends) from the origin E = x, D = y
> Witness: w1 2 entries (rub, end), 51 lines.
$B118 c DrawCharAt ; Draw character A at pixel (L, H) with a 4-pixel offset (two half bytes a line)
> Below $20: a space. a-z from Font, digits from Font+$D0, others from CHAR_SET. Writes (not XOR) the glyph's left and right nibbles into two bytes, so a cell 16 pixels wide shows the glyph centred. Witness: w1 63, frames 12023-13163.
$B11E DrawCharLetter
$B12D DrawCharDigit
$B13C DrawCharRom
$B141 DrawCharGlyph
$B150 DrawCharLines
$B167 b CharacterGrid 14 ; The name-entry grid: 3 rows of 14 cells
> Read by DrawCharacterGrid and PickCell (w1 45 reads). Row 0 a-n, row 1 o-z, space, "."; row 2 0-9, $0C $0C ("rub", two cells), $0D $0D ("end").
@res text/CharacterGrid.bin
$B191 b RubLines 3 ; The word "rub": lines (dx, dy, length) from (x $B7, y $90), $FF ends
> Read by DrawLineList (w1 97 reads).
@res graphics/RubLines.bin
$B1DA b EndLines 3 ; The word "end": lines (dx, dy, length) from (x $D7, y $90), $FF ends
> Read by DrawLineList (w1 102 reads).
@res graphics/EndLines.bin
$B226 b RepeatDelay ; The name entry's auto-repeat counter: two nibbles, high = the delay, low = the countdown ($70 at rest)
> Written and read by NameEntry (w1 103 reads).
$B227 t NameBuffer ; The name being typed: 7 characters ("_" = empty)
> Written by NameEntry and PickCell, read by DrawName and copied to NewEntryName1 / NewEntryName2 by GameOverHallOfFame (w1 53 reads).

@module menu ; The main menu, the variations page, redefine keys, the settings and the menu's timeout
@about MainMenu $B22E (called from $8037 after every restart) draws the options page (MenuTexts $B464, white on blue), the current variation line, the key names and a tick at the chosen players, pockets and control, then reads keys through the ROM (KEY-SCAN, K-TEST): 1 / 2 players, S / L pockets, K / I / J / C the control (keyboard, Interface 2, Kempston, cursor), B begins the game (MainMenu returns), V the variations page, E the editor ($C042), R redefine keys. 1500 frames without a key (MenuTimeout $B8C1, 30 s) run the attract sequence $B8C5 and then the menu again (w1 frame 16300). Each key clicks (BEEPER, HL $03E8, DE $14).
@about With a joystick chosen, its fire also begins the game: Kempston is polled every frame; for Interface 2 and cursor (whose fire is a key) $8106 is asked when a key the menu does not know is pressed. Choosing J probes the Kempston port: if $8106 reads anything while J is set, the old choice stays (no interface, a floating bus).
@about A hidden toggle: WaitNoKey $B38E, run at every menu redraw and after every key, flips InfiniteLives $B8C4 when CAPS SHIFT, SPACE, 1 and 0 are held together (TestCheatKeys $B3B8); $9754 then skips the loss of a life. No recording flipped it (known only from the code; $B3A1 never ran).
@about The text records here and everywhere the print routine $9E65 is used: row, column, attribute (bit 7 = double width: every character two cells), length, then the characters, no end marker. The characters are ASCII: a-z from Font $DEF4, 0-9 from Font+$D0, $7F the copyright sign at $9F2E, anything else (space, punctuation) from CHAR_SET $3D00 ($9E7C).
$B22E c MainMenu ; The main menu: draw the page, then take keys until B (or joystick fire) begins the game
> Clears the pixels, attributes $0F, border 1, BORDCR $08. Prints the records of MenuTexts $B464, the variation line (VariationStandard $B5D0 / Variation1Line $B5E3 / Variation2Line $B5F4 by Variation $B8C3), the key line (ShowKeys $B605), and XorTick $B89C at rows Players + 3, Pockets + 7 and Control + 10. Then MenuKeys $B293.
> Callers: $8037 (every restart from $8013), the ends of the timeout ($B2C3, $B6D4) and M on the variations page ($B6F7). Returns (to $803A, the game) on B, or on fire. Witness: game 2 (frames 6060, 9809), w1 2, f1 2, p2 2, v1 2, m1 4.
$B252 MenuTextLoop
$B263 MenuVariationLine
$B275 MenuDrawSettings
$B293 MenuKeys ; MenuTimeout = 1500 ($05DC), wait until no key, then a key a frame
$B29C MenuKeyLoop ; Kempston: $8106 every frame, fire begins
$B2AD MenuReadKey ; no key: MenuTimeout - 1; at 0 the attract sequence, then MainMenu again
$B2C6 MenuKey ; click; "1" / "2" players
$B2D9 MenuPlayers ; XorTick off at the old row, on at the new
$B2F5 MenuPocketKeys ; "S" small (0) / "L" large (1)
$B2FD MenuPockets
$B303 MenuPocketsSet
$B31D MenuControlKeys ; "K" 0, "I" 1, "J" 2, "C" 3
$B334 MenuControl ; J: Control = 2, $8106; InputState not 0 (port bits 0-4, or BREAK): Control back to the old value
$B357 MenuControlSet
$B36A MenuOtherKeys ; "B" begin, "V" variations, "E" the editor, "R" redefine; another key: with a joystick, $8106 fire begins
$B38E c WaitNoKey ; Wait until no key is held; flip InfiniteLives if CAPS SHIFT, SPACE, 1 and 0 are held
> A frame each: KEY-SCAN, K-TEST; returns when no key. While a key is held TestCheatKeys $B3B8 is asked: all four held complements InfiniteLives $B8C4 and waits for the release ($B3A8).
> Witness: game 73, w1 14, f1 14, p2 24, v1 36, m1 202 calls; $B3B8 asked game 70, m1 176 times; the flip ($B3A1) never in the case's recordings; in the experiment f1c at frame 510 (docs 10.10).
$B39C WaitNoKeyHeld
$B3A8 WaitNoKeyRelease
$B3B8 c TestCheatKeys ; Z when CAPS SHIFT, SPACE, 1 and 0 are all held
> ORs bit 0 of the half-rows $FEFE (CAPS SHIFT), $7FFE (SPACE), $F7FE (1) and $EFFE (0) through IN A,(C): an IN from port $FE (see also $8216 and $822E; doc 2.1 lists the reads). Witness: game 70, w1 12, f1 12, p2 21, v1 30, m1 176.
$B3D2 c RedefineKeys ; "R": ask for the left, right and fire keys, store their K-TEST codes
> RedefinePrompt $B3F3 with PromptLeft $B407, PromptRight $B426, PromptFire $B445 into LeftKey $B8BE, RightKey $B8BF, FireKey $B8C0; ShowKeys; back to MenuKeys. Witness: m1 2 (frames 5462-5860, Q W P, then M, SYMBOL SHIFT, SPACE again; screens m1 frames 5550, 5860).
$B3F3 c RedefinePrompt ; Print the prompt at HL, wait for no key, then for a key: A = its K-TEST code
> Any key is taken, CAPS SHIFT alone ($27) and SYMBOL SHIFT alone ($18) too. Witness: m1 6, frames 5462-6090.
$B3F9 RedefineWaitKey
$B407 t PromptLeft ; "select key for left ?" (row 21, column 2, attribute $0F, 27 characters, space-padded)
> The three prompts, read by RedefinePrompt (m1 186 reads); each covers the key line at row 21.
$B426 PromptRight ; "select key for right ?"
$B445 PromptFire ; "select key for fire ?"
@res text/PromptLeft.bin
$B464 w MenuTexts 2 ; The menu page's 15 text records, then a zero word
> Read by MainMenu (every recording, m1 128 reads).
$B484 t MenuTitle ; row 0 column 9, 14: "- video pool -"
> The records of MenuTexts, all attribute $0F (screen m1 frame 4620); read by MainMenu in every recording.
$B496 MenuSelect ; at (2, 0), 16 chars
$B4AA MenuOnePlayer ; at (4, 2), 18 chars
$B4C0 MenuTwoPlayers ; at (5, 2), 18 chars
$B4D6 MenuSmallPockets ; at (7, 2), 16 chars
$B4EA MenuLargePockets ; at (8, 2), 16 chars
$B4FE MenuKeyboard ; at (10, 2), 11 chars
$B50D MenuInterface2 ; at (11, 2), 25 chars
$B52A MenuKempston ; at (12, 2), 20 chars
$B542 MenuCursor ; at (13, 2), 18 chars
$B558 MenuBegin ; at (15, 2), 13 chars
$B569 MenuVariations ; at (17, 2), 18 chars
$B57F MenuEdit ; at (18, 2), 13 chars
$B590 MenuRedefine ; at (19, 2), 24 chars
$B5AC MenuCopyright ; (23, 0), 32
$B5D0 VariationStandard ; (15, 16), 15
$B5E3 Variation1Line ; (15, 16), 13
$B5F4 Variation2Line ; (15, 16), 13; no build reads it
@res text/MenuTitle.bin
$B605 c ShowKeys ; Fill the key names into KeysLine and print it
> KeyName $B626 for LeftKey to $B668, RightKey to $B672, FireKey to $B67B, then KeysLine $B65F through $9E65 (a jump). The default line: "left='m' right=s/s fire=' '". Witness: game 2, w1 2, f1 2, p2 2, v1 2, m1 6 (frames 4506-8004).
$B626 c KeyName ; Three characters at HL naming the K-TEST code A
> $0D "ent", $18 "s/s" (SYMBOL SHIFT), $27 "c/s" (CAPS SHIFT), else the character in quotes, letters lower-cased (+$20). Witness: game 6 ($B63C 4 quoted, $B64D 2 s/s); m1 18; "ent" and "c/s" by no recording.
$B63C KeyNameQuoted
$B644 KeyNameEnter
$B64D KeyNameSymbol
$B656 KeyNameCaps
$B65F t KeysLine ; "left=    right=    fire=   " (row 21, column 2, attribute $0F, 27 characters); the names filled in
> Written by ShowKeys, read by $9E65 (every recording).
$B668 KeysLineLeft ; the left key's name (3 characters)
$B66B ; " right="
$B672 KeysLineRight ; the right key's name
$B675 ; " fire="
$B67B KeysLineFire ; the fire key's name
@res text/KeysLine.bin
$B67E c VariationsPage ; "V": the variations page; S / 1 / 2 choose, B begins, M back to the menu
> Clears the pixels (the attributes stay), prints VariationTexts $B731, ticks the variation (TickVariation $B71E), then like MenuKeys: MenuTimeout 1500, Kempston fire polled, a key a frame: S Variation 0, 1 variation 1, 2 variation 2 (ticks moved), B returns (the game begins), M MainMenu; another key with a joystick asks $8106 for fire. Note $B6BC loops while KEY-SCAN reports more than two keys.
> Witness: v1 2 (frames 622-2595), m1 1 (5102-5372); screens m1 frame 5190, v1 frame 700.
$B68E VariationsTextLoop
$B69F VariationsTick
$B6A2 VariationsKeys
$B6AB VariationsKeyLoop
$B6BC VariationsReadKey
$B6D7 VariationsKey
$B70B VariationsSet
$B71E c TickVariation ; XorTick at the row of Variation: 4 (standard), 7 (1), 11 (2)
> Witness: v1 6, m1 7.
$B72D TickVariationRow
$B731 w VariationTexts 2 ; The variations page's 13 text records, then a zero word
> Read by VariationsPage (v1 56, m1 28 reads).
$B74D t VariationsTitle ; row 0 column 6, 19: "- game variations -"
> The records of VariationTexts, attribute $0F, read by VariationsPage in v1 and m1.
$B764 VariationsSelect ; at (2, 0), 16 chars
$B778 VariationsS1 ; at (4, 2), 29 chars
$B799 VariationsS2 ; at (5, 5), 22 chars
$B7B3 Variations11 ; at (7, 2), 27 chars
$B7D2 Variations12 ; at (8, 5), 26 chars
$B7F0 Variations13 ; at (9, 5), 22 chars
$B80A Variations21 ; at (11, 2), 25 chars
$B827 Variations22 ; at (12, 5), 25 chars
$B844 Variations23 ; at (13, 5), 24 chars
$B860 Variations24 ; at (14, 5), 23 chars
$B87B VariationsBegin ; at (17, 2), 13 chars
$B88C VariationsMenu ; at (19, 2), 12 chars
@res text/VariationsTitle.bin
$B89C c XorTick ; XOR a tick at the left of text row A (x 5, y 8 A + 4)
> Two one-pixel steps down-right, then seven up-right (XorLine $93B3, length 1). Drawing twice erases: the menus move a tick by drawing it at the old row and the new. Witness: game 6, w1 6, f1 6, p2 8, v1 12, m1 39.
$B8A8 TickDown
$B8B2 TickUp
$B8BB b Players ; The menu's settings: first the players, 1 or 2 (keys 1 / 2), then pockets, control, the three keys, the timeout, the variation, the cheat
> One byte each (MenuTimeout a word). Readers and writers in the comments. The defaults in the image: one player, large pockets, keyboard, keys M / SYMBOL SHIFT / SPACE, standard game.
> Players is read by GameOverHallOfFame, NameEntryPage and the play code ($97AB, $97D0, $98D5, $9B36, $9C15, $9C7B)
$B8BC Pockets ; 0 small, 1 large (keys S / L); read by the table drawing and the pocket tests ($8B71, $8C74, $9256-$9330)
$B8BD Control ; 0 keyboard, 1 Interface 2, 2 Kempston, 3 cursor (keys K / I / J / C); the handler index of $8106; the editor sets 3 while it runs if it is 0 ($C042, $C417, $C44E)
$B8BE LeftKey ; K-TEST codes of the keyboard control's keys, read by $8123; set by RedefineKeys
$B8BF RightKey
$B8C0 FireKey
$B8C1 MenuTimeout ; frames left before the attract sequence (1500 at each key)
$B8C3 Variation ; 0 standard, 1 numeric order, 2 own pockets; read by $8C2F, $9BFA, $9C00
$B8C4 InfiniteLives ; non-zero: no life is lost ($9754); flipped by WaitNoKey

@module presents ; The presents page: the OCP banner rolling in, "presents", the attract loop
@about PresentsPage $B8C5 is the game's first screen (called by GameStart $8000 before IM 2 is set up) and the attract sequence the menu falls into after its timeout ($B293, $B67E). It runs BannerRollIn $B96C (the magenta OCP banner slides in row by row), prints "presents", flips the banner's letters from OCP to POOL (LogoFlip $BB13), slides in "video" (VideoSlideIn $BC9E), lets a pen write "written by" and James Hutchby's signature (SignatureWrite $BD0C), prints "press any key to continue." and starts again. Every wait goes through PresentsFrame $B91B: one HALT and a key test; a key reloads the stack pointer saved at entry and returns from PresentsPage to its caller, whatever depth the sequence was at.
@about The banner's drawing has its own pixel-address routine and 4-pixel XOR blocks: a row moves right 4 pixels a frame by XORing one block at the start and one past the end of each ink run (BannerRowEdges $BA0A), so a row costs two blocks per run, not a redraw.
$B8C5 c PresentsPage ; The presents page and the attract loop: banner, "presents", OCP to POOL, "video", the signature, "press any key"
> Saves SP in PresentsSP $BD09 (the abort target of PresentsFrame $B91B), draws the banner (BannerRollIn $B96C), prints "presents" (PresentsTexts $B936, row 16 column 12) for 75 frames, blanks it, flips the letters (LogoFlip $BB13), slides in "video" (VideoSlideIn $BC9E), waits 50 frames, runs the pen (SignatureWrite $BD0C), waits 25 frames, prints "press any key to continue." (row 23) and waits 500 frames (PresentsLongWait $B90F). Then, if PresentsDone $BD0B is zero (the first call, from GameStart $8000, which sets it to $FF only after the return), it starts again at once; otherwise it calls $A8FD, waits 500 frames more and starts again.
> Called by GameStart $8000 ($800B) and by the menu ($B293 after its timeout, $B67E). Returns only through a key (PresentsAbort $B931). Witness (l1sum.py B8C5): entered in every build; game 5556-6051 (a key on "presents" at 6051), f1 / p2 / v1 0-494, w1 twice (0-494 and the attract after the menu's timeout at 16 160-16 377), m1 0-4129 with two complete loops (the 500-frame wait PresentsLongWait ran at 1237 and 3474, then JP back, since PresentsDone was 0); the $A8FD branch ($B906) ran in no build.
$B8D4 PresentsHold1 ; 75 frames with "presents" up
$B8E7 PresentsHold2 ; 50 frames after "video"
$B8F1 PresentsHold3 ; 25 frames after the signature
$B90F c PresentsLongWait ; Wait 500 frames, a key aborts
> 500 calls of PresentsFrame $B91B (BC = 500). m1 ran it twice (frames 1237 and 3474, 1000 loop passes); no other build reached the end of the sequence.
$B912 PresentsLongWaitLoop
$B91B c PresentsFrame ; Wait one frame (HALT); a key returns from PresentsPage to its caller
> HALT, then the ROM's KEY-SCAN $028E and K-TEST $031E: no key (Z and K-TEST $FF) returns with all registers kept; a key (or more than two keys, NZ from KEY-SCAN) reloads SP from PresentsSP $BD09 and RETs, which returns from PresentsPage $B8C5 to the caller that saved it. Used by every wait of the attract sequence (BannerRollIn, LogoFlip, VideoSlideIn and the pen's steps in SignatureCommand $BD23).
> Witness: game 499 calls 5559-6060, w1 1084, m1 4353 (2-4500); the abort PresentsAbort ran once in every build (game 6051 / 6060, w1, f1, p2, v1 at 494-500, m1 at 4500, the SPACE of tests/inputs/m1.txt).
$B931 PresentsAbort ; a key: back to PresentsPage's caller
$B936 b PresentsTexts 12 ; The page's texts in the print format of $9E65: row, column, attribute, length, characters
> Three records for the print routine $9E65 (row, column, attribute, length, then the characters): "presents" (row 16, column 12, attribute $38), eight spaces over it, and "press any key to continue." (row 23, column 3, attribute $38, 26 characters). Read by PresentsPage $B8C5; the first 24 bytes in every build, the last record only in m1 (the only build that reached the end of the sequence).
$B942 PresentsBlank ; eight spaces
$B94E PressAnyKey ; "press any key to continue."
@res text/PresentsTexts.bin
$B96C c BannerRollIn ; Clear the screen and roll the magenta OCP banner in from the left, row by row (417 frames)
> Clears the display file, border white (OUT $07), attributes $38 (black on white), rows 4-12 $3B (magenta ink). Then the banner: HL is the x of the moving row as a signed 16-bit number, starting at -240 off the left edge; B is the pixel line of the top moving row (from 32), DE the run-length row of BannerData $BA86. Each frame (PresentsFrame $B91B) it calls BannerDrawRows $B9E0 and moves x 4 pixels right; when the top row reaches x 8 its line is done: B goes one line down, every second line DE moves to the next data row (each data row is two pixel lines), and x goes back 20 pixels for the row below. It ends at line 104 ($68), 72 lines (36 data rows), and sets the attribute $7B (bright) on rows 4-12, columns 1-30.
> Witness (l1sum.py B96C B9E0): 1 call per presents page, game 5556-5976; BannerDrawRows 417 calls each (game 5560-5976). WALKTHROUGH 6.1 m1 frame 300 shows the staircase of rows half way in.
$B99B BannerFrameLoop ; one frame: draw, x + 4
$B9B8 BannerSkipRow ; DE past one data row (runs summing to 240 pixels)
$B9C1 BannerNextRow ; the next line's x: 20 pixels behind
$B9D3 BannerBrightRows ; attribute $7B on rows 4-12, columns 1-30
$B9D7 BannerBrightLoop
$B9E0 c BannerDrawRows ; Move every row from line B downwards that is on screen: 4 pixels right
> For the line B and each line below (x 20 pixels further left each, DE advancing every second line as in BannerRollIn $B96C): BannerRowEdges $BA0A, while the row's x is within -256..255 (H = 0 or $FF) and the line is above 104. Registers kept. game 417 calls, 4748 rows (BannerRowEdges).
$B9E3 BannerRowLoop
$B9F8 BannerSameRow ; odd line: the same data row again
$B9F9 BannerRowClip ; stop when x is past -256..255 or the line reaches 104
$BA01 BannerRowNext
$BA06 BannerRowsDone
$BA0A c BannerRowEdges ; One banner row 4 pixels right: XOR a block at the start and one at the end of each ink run
> DE = the row's runs in BannerData $BA86, HL = x, B = the line. For each run byte (bit 7 = ink, bits 0-6 = half the length): an ink run gets BannerEdgeBlock $BA31 at x and at x + length, which clears the 4 pixels the run leaves and sets the 4 it enters (the row was drawn 4 pixels to the left on the frame before). Runs until the lengths add up to 240 pixels (C counts down from $F0). game 4748 calls, 18 476 runs.
$BA0C BannerRunLoop
$BA1C BannerRunEnd ; the block at the run's end
$BA20 BannerRunSkip ; x + length
$BA27 BannerRunNext
$BA31 c BannerEdgeBlock ; XOR the 4-pixel block at x (HL signed) on line B, if x is on screen
> HL = x (signed), B = the line. x 0-255: BannerXorBlock $BA54 at x; x -4..-1: the block is drawn as far as it reaches onto the screen (x + 4 with B = that count). Registers kept. game 21 112 calls.
$BA3E BannerEdgeRight ; x negative: try x + 4
$BA45 BannerEdgeCheck
$BA4D BannerEdgeXor
$BA50 BannerEdgeDone
$BA54 c BannerXorBlock ; XOR 4 pixels (a nibble) at x E, line D: B = 0 draws nothing
> The screen byte from PresentsPixelAddress $BA6C, XORed with $F0 when x AND 7 is 0 and $0F otherwise (the banner's x is always a multiple of 4). game 10 127 calls.
$BA69 BannerXorNibble
$BA6C c PresentsPixelAddress ; HL = the display-file address of pixel line H, x L (the byte; the caller adds the line within the cell)
> H = y, L = x in: L = (y AND $38) * 4 + x / 8, H = $40 + (y AND $C0) / 8. The caller adds y AND 7 to H. Used by BannerXorBlock $BA54 and VideoSlideIn $BC9E. game 9808 calls; the same code as PenPixelAddress $BFB2.
$BA86 b BannerData 16 ; The OCP banner: 37 rows of run lengths, 240 pixels each (bit 7 ink, bits 0-6 half the length)
> One byte a run: bit 7 set = magenta ink, clear = paper; bits 0-6 = half the run's length in pixels. A row ends when its runs add up to 240 pixels: $F8 alone is a full ink row, $78 a full paper row. The letters O, C and P are the paper gaps in the ink stripes (WALKTHROUGH 6.1, m1 frame 300). Read by BannerRowEdges $BA0A (and BannerRollIn $B96C, BannerDrawRows $B9E0 to skip rows); all 141 bytes in every build.
@res graphics/BannerData.bin

@module attract ; The attract sequence's letters: OCP turning into POOL, "video" sliding in
@about LogoFlip $BB13 turns the banner's letters about their vertical axes: in 20 steps the O, C and P (gaps in the magenta stripes) narrow to nothing, then in 20 steps P, O, O, L widen in their place. Each letter is a list of edges per stripe row (FlipLetterO $BB98 and the next three); FlipEdge $BC11 XORs only the strip between an edge's width at this step and at the next one, taken from the cosine tables FlipWidths16 $BC74 / FlipWidths24 $BC89.
@about VideoSlideIn $BC9E prints "video" above the banner with the font $DEF4 doubled in width (16 pixels a letter), each letter scrolling into its cell 2 pixels a step.
$BB13 c LogoFlip ; Turn the banner's OCP into POOL: 20 steps narrowing O, C, P, then 20 steps widening P, O, O, L
> Step C 0-19 with B = 0: FlipLetter $BB74 for O at x 63, C at x 127, P at x 191 (line 47, DE = $2F3F / $2F7F / $2FBF), one frame each (PresentsFrame $B91B). Then C 20 down to 1 with B = 1 (widening): P at x 41, O at 101, O at 161, L at 221.
> Witness (l1sum.py BB13 BB17 BB42): once per presents page; game 6051-6059 (7 narrowing steps, then the key), w1 2 runs (494- and 16 323), m1 3 runs of 20 + 20 steps (494-4055). WALKTHROUGH 6.1: m1 frame 300 shows OCP, frame 900 POOL.
$BB17 FlipNarrowLoop ; O C P narrowing, C = 0..19
$BB42 FlipWidenLoop ; P O O L widening, C = 20..1
$BB74 c FlipLetter ; One step of one letter: FlipEdge for each edge record of the list at IX, relative to D (line), E (x centre)
> IX = a letter's list of 3-byte records (dx, dy, kind) ending $FF; B = the phase (0 narrowing, 1 widening), C = the step. For each record: E + dx, D + dy and kind + phase go to FlipEdge $BC11. Registers kept but IX. game 210 records, m1 4800.
$BB98 b FlipLetterO 3 ; The letter O: 12 edge records (dx, dy, kind), $FF
> Each letter: per stripe row (dy 0, 8, ... 40, the six stripe pairs) a record for the left edge (dx 0) and one for the right edge (dx 1, kind bit 1 set). Kind: bit 1 = the right edge, bit 2 = the 24-pixel width table, bit 3 = both tables (two edges drawn, 16 and 24); bit 0 is the phase LogoFlip adds. Read by FlipLetter $BB74: all 121 bytes in w1 and m1 (game, f1, p2, v1 stop at the key during the narrowing, so the L is never read there).
@res graphics/FlipLetters.bin
$BBBC ; end of O
$BBBD FlipLetterC ; The letter C: 10 records, $FF
$BBDB ; end of C
$BBDC FlipLetterP ; The letter P: 10 records, $FF
$BBFA ; end of P
$BBFB FlipLetterL ; The letter L: 7 records, $FF
$BC10 ; end of L
$BC11 c FlipEdge ; XOR the strip an edge moves by between step C and the next on four lines (D-2, D-1, D+2, D+3)
> E = the letter's centre x, D = the stripe line, B = kind + phase, C = the step. Bit 3 of B: both widths (FlipEdge again with bit 2 set, then clear). Otherwise the width w = FlipWidths16 $BC74 [C] (or FlipWidths24 $BC89 with bit 2), compared with the entry after it (phase 0) or before it (phase 1); no change, nothing drawn. The strip of |difference| pixels is placed left (bit 1 clear) or right of the centre at the edge's position and XORed by the span routine $93B3 (B = length) on the lines D-2, D-1, D+2 and D+3: the two two-line stripes of one letter row.
> Witness: game 432 calls, w1 3628, m1 10 020 (6052-4055).
$BC1E FlipEdgeWide ; bit 2: the 24-pixel table
$BC21 FlipEdgeStep
$BC2E FlipEdgeWiden ; phase 1: compare with the entry before
$BC2F FlipEdgeCompare
$BC37 FlipEdgeWidth
$BC43 FlipEdgeLeft
$BC47 FlipEdgeRight
$BC4C FlipEdgeXor
$BC63 FlipEdgeBoth ; bit 3: both tables
$BC74 b FlipWidths16 7 ; Half-widths of a turning letter: 16 cos(4.5 k) for k = 0..20 (16 ... 1, 0)
> 21 bytes 16, 16, 16, 16, 15, 15, 14, 14, 13, 12, 11, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 1, 0: the projected half-width of a 16-pixel half letter turned by 4.5 degrees a step (16 cos 45 = 11.3, entry 10 = 11). Read by FlipEdge $BC11; all in w1 and m1.
@res tables/FlipWidths.bin
$BC89 FlipWidths24 ; the same for 24 pixels: 24 cos(4.5 k), 24 ... 2, 0
$BC9E c VideoSlideIn ; Print "video" above the banner, letters 16 pixels wide sliding into their cells 2 pixels a step
> Eight steps C = 7..0, three frames each (PresentsFrame $B91B). Each step prints the five letters of VideoText $BD04 at line 24 ($18), x 8 + 24 i: each font row (Font $DEF4, letter - 'a') doubled to 16 bits, shifted left by 2 C bits, written as two screen bytes over the old ones (no XOR), so each letter enters its cell from the right.
> Witness (l1sum.py BC9E BCA0): w1 once (16 323-16 347), m1 three times (581-4079); in game, f1, p2, v1 a key came first.
$BCA0 VideoStepLoop ; C = 7..0
$BCA2 VideoStepWait ; three frames
$BCB3 VideoCharLoop ; five letters
$BCC8 VideoLineLoop ; eight lines
$BCD0 VideoDoubleLoop ; each bit twice
$BCE2 VideoShiftLoop ; shift left 2 C bits
$BCEC VideoStore
$BD04 b VideoText 5 ; "video", the five letters of VideoSlideIn
> Read by VideoSlideIn $BC9E (w1, m1).
@res text/VideoText.bin
$BD09 b PresentsSP ; SP at PresentsPage's entry: PresentsAbort $B931 reloads it (written and read once in every build)
$BD0B PresentsDone ; 0 while GameStart $8000's first presents page runs ($8006), $FF after it ($800E): PresentsPage then also calls $A8FD (read twice in m1, both 0)

@module signature ; The pen that writes "written by" and the signature
@about SignatureWrite $BD0C runs the pen script SignatureScript $DA80: one command a byte, a pen step a frame. The pen is a sprite of three parts drawn relative to its point (x E, y D): a nib of ten spans (PenNib $BFCC), a 45-degree shaft 44 lines long copied from PenShaftImage $BFFE, and an end cap of six spans (PenCap $BFEB). Each step erases the pen (PenErase $BE40), plots the ink pixel at (x, y - 1) when the pen is down, moves one pixel and draws the pen again (PenDraw $BE2F).
@about The span drawer PenSpan $BF66 and the shaft routine PenShaftBlock $BEDC are self-modifying: PenDraw writes OR (HL) into the three NOPs of PenSpan and a JR 0 into PenShaftBlock (copy the shaft bytes); PenErase writes XOR (HL) and a JR to PenShaftClear $BF41 (zero the shaft bytes). The shaft copy jumps into one of four 16-byte-aligned copy loops (1-4 bytes a line, PenShaftCopy1 $BF03 .. PenShaftCopy4 $BF33) by the bytes left to the screen's right edge.
$BD0C c SignatureWrite ; Run the pen script SignatureScript: "written by" and James Hutchby's signature, one pen step a frame
> Pen up (PenIsDown $C03F = 0), PenBand $C03E = 0, IX = SignatureScript $DA80, the pen at x 16, y 128 (DE = $8010), PenDraw $BE2F, then SignatureCommand $BD23 until the end command, which drops this loop's return address and leaves through PenErase $BE40 back to PresentsPage $B8C5.
> Witness (l1sum.py BD0C BD23 BE05): m1 only, 3 runs (655-4498), 828 commands and 1484 steps: two whole scripts (312 commands, 557 steps each, by the decoder in 05-editor-notes.md) and a third cut by the SPACE at 4500. WALKTHROUGH 6.1: m1 frame 900 (the pen after "written by"), 1500 (the signature done).
$BD1E SignatureLoop
$BD23 c SignatureCommand ; One command of the pen script at IX: end, pen up, pen down, dot, or a move of n steps
> Byte b at IX. High nibble zero, a command (IX + 1): 0 end (pops the caller's return, JP PenErase $BE40), 1 pen up (PenIsDown $C03F = 0), 2 pen down (= 1), 3 dot: the next two bytes dx, dy (signed) are added to the pen point for one PenPlot $BF59 (the dot of an i, a full stop), the point does not move; 4-15 do nothing (SigCmdOther, never run). High nibble not zero, a move (IX + 1): bits 7-6 dy, bits 5-4 dx (each 0, +1, -2, -1 by sign extension; the script uses 0, +1, -1), bits 3-0 n steps. Each step: one frame (PresentsFrame $B91B, a key aborts), PenErase, PenStep $BE05 (plot at (x, y - 1) if the pen is down, then x + dx, y + dy), PenDraw.
> The band test: with the pen's y at 96 or more (the whole script keeps the pen at y 121-147) the step is plain. For y below 96 other paths slow the pen down: y 40-95 ($28-$5F) through PenDraw, PenDelay $BE24 and PenErase, y below 40 through SigBandSlow (PenDelay) and PenBand $C03E records the pen leaving the band (1 above, 2 below) for a pause (SigBandExitAbove, a 300-loop wait) on the next move: no build ran any of them (SigBandBelow .. SigBandSlow, l1sum.py: not executed); known only from the code.
> Witness: m1 828 calls (655-4499) = two whole scripts of 312 commands (218 moves, 30 pen up, 30 pen down, 33 dots, the end) and 204 of the third, cut by a key; 582 moves (SigMove), 1485 step frames.
$BD39 SigCmdPenUp
$BD42 SigCmdPenDown
$BD4C SigCmdDot ; dx, dy: one pixel, the point stays
$BD64 SigCmdOther ; commands 4-15: nothing (never run)
$BD65 SigMove ; H = dy, L = dx from bits 7-6 / 5-4, C = n from bits 3-0
$BD9A SigMoveStep ; one frame a step
$BDC1 SigBandBelow ; never run: y left the band downwards
$BDCA SigBandCheck ; never run
$BDD5 SigBandExitBelow ; never run
$BDDE SigBandExitAbove ; never run: a pause of 300 loops
$BDEC SigBandPause
$BDF4 SigBandSlow ; never run: the step delayed by PenDelay
$BDF7 SigStepDraw ; erase the pen, step, draw it
$BDFD SigStepNext
$BE05 c PenStep ; One step of the pen: plot (E, D - 1) if the pen is down, then D + H, E + L; C - 1
> C = the steps left, H / L = dy / dx. With PenIsDown $C03F set, PenPlot $BF59 at (x, y - 1), the pixel just above the nib's tip. B = 1: one step per call. m1 1484 calls.
$BE0A PenStepLoop
$BE18 PenStepMove
$BE24 c PenDelay ; A busy wait of 900 loops (the band's slow step; never run)
> Called from SigBandSlow $BDF4 only; no build ran it.
$BE28 PenDelayLoop
$BE2F c PenDraw ; Draw the pen at (E, D): spans OR, shaft copied
> Writes JR 0 at PenShaftBlock $BEDC (copy path) and OR (HL) ($B6) into PenSpanOp1-3 ($BF93, $BFA1, $BFAE), then PenRender $BE50. m1 1487 calls.
$BE40 c PenErase ; Erase the pen at (E, D): spans XOR, shaft cleared
> Writes JR $63 at PenShaftBlock $BEDC (to PenShaftClear $BF41) and XOR (HL) ($AE) into PenSpanOp1-3, then PenRender: PenShape $BE5A with HL, BC, DE kept. m1 1486 calls.
$BE50 PenRender ; PenShape with HL, BC, DE kept (PenDraw jumps here)
$BE5A c PenShape ; The pen's three parts at (E, D): nib spans, shaft from (E + 5, D + 10), cap spans
> PenSpans $BE79 with PenNib $BFCC; returns at once if x + 5 passes 255 or y + 10 reaches 192; PenShaftPos $C040 = (x + 5, y + 10), PenShaft $BE9F; then PenSpans with PenCap $BFEB. m1 2973 calls.
$BE79 c PenSpans ; Draw the span list at HL relative to (E, D): records dx, dy, length, $FF end; clipped right and bottom
> Each record: the span at (x + dx, y + dy) of the given length by PenSpan $BF66 (OR or XOR as set), the length cut at the right edge; a span past the bottom (y 192) or past x 255 ends the list. m1 5946 calls, 40 732 spans.
$BE96 PenSpansDraw
$BE9D PenSpansClip
$BE9F c PenShaft ; The pen's shaft: 44 lines down-right from PenShaftPos, in blocks that end on character-cell lines
> From PenShaftPos $C040: blocks of up to 8 lines (the first to the end of its character row), each by PenShaftBlock $BEDC; x and y both advance by the block's lines (45 degrees); stops at the bottom of the screen or when x wraps. 44 lines in all ($2C). m1 2973 calls.
$BEB6 PenShaftLoop
$BED9 PenShaftLast
$BEDC c PenShaftBlock ; B lines of the shaft at PenShaftPos: copy from PenShaftImage (draw) or zero them (erase), self-modified
> The JR at $BEDC is the switch: displacement 0 (PenDraw) runs the copy, $63 (PenErase) jumps to PenShaftClear $BF41. Copy: HL = PenShaftImage $BFFE + 4 (x AND 7), DE the screen; the bytes to copy are 1-4 (fewer at columns 29-31: the bytes left to the right edge, at most 3, plus one), written as 16 times (that count - 1) into the displacement of the JR at PenShaftJump $BF01, which lands on PenShaftCopy1-4 ($BF03, $BF13, $BF23, $BF33). Each line takes the next 4-byte row of the image (the shaft moves one pixel right a line). m1 18 215 calls; only PenShaftCopy4 ran (the pen never came near the right edge).
$BEDE PenShaftCopyPath
$BEED PenShaftWidth ; bytes to the right edge, at most 3
$BEFA PenShaftBytes ; x 16 into the JR below
$BF01 PenShaftJump ; JR to PenShaftCopy1 + 16 (bytes - 1)
$BF03 PenShaftCopy1 ; one byte a line (never run)
$BF0D b PenShaftPad1 ; Six zero bytes that align PenShaftCopy2 at PenShaftCopy1 + 16
> Never read or executed (l1sum.py --reads BF0D-BF32: no build).
$BF13 c PenShaftCopy2 ; The shaft copy, two bytes a line (a target of PenShaftJump; never run)
> LDI twice, DE back, the next screen line, the next image row; DJNZ. No build ran it.
$BF1F b PenShaftPad2 ; Four zero bytes of alignment
$BF23 c PenShaftCopy3 ; The shaft copy, three bytes a line (never run)
> As PenShaftCopy2 $BF13 with three LDIs. No build ran it.
$BF31 b PenShaftPad3 ; Two zero bytes of alignment
$BF33 c PenShaftCopy4 ; The shaft copy, four bytes a line
> PUSH DE, four LDIs, POP DE, INC D; DJNZ. m1 64 168 lines.
$BF41 c PenShaftClear ; Erase B lines of the shaft: zero the bytes up to the right edge (1-4)
> The target of PenShaftBlock's JR $63 when PenErase set it. m1 9105 calls, 64 128 lines.
$BF4B PenClearBytes
$BF4C PenClearLine
$BF4E PenClearByte
$BF59 c PenPlot ; Plot one pixel at (E, D) with OR (sets PenSpan's mode to OR, B = 1)
> Writes OR (HL) into PenSpanOp1-3 and falls into PenSpan $BF66 with one pixel. Called by PenStep $BE05 and the dot command SigCmdDot $BD4C; m1 1105 calls.
$BF66 c PenSpan ; A horizontal span of B pixels at (E, D), ORed or XORed by the self-modified PenSpanOp1-3
> Builds the left partial byte, the full bytes and the right partial byte; the NOPs at PenSpanOp1 $BF93, PenSpanOp2 $BFA1 and PenSpanOp3 $BFAE hold OR (HL) or XOR (HL) (PenDraw / PenErase / PenPlot). m1 41 837 calls.
$BF7B PenSpanShort ; under 8 pixels: the mask
$BF82 PenSpanLong
$BF85 PenSpanShift ; shift the mask by x AND 7
$BF8A PenSpanShiftLoop
$BF8F PenSpanShiftNext
$BF92 PenSpanFirst
$BF93 PenSpanOp1 ; NOP / OR (HL) / XOR (HL)
$BF96 PenSpanMiddle
$BFA1 PenSpanOp2 ; NOP / OR (HL) / XOR (HL)
$BFA6 PenSpanTail
$BFA8 PenSpanTailLoop
$BFAE PenSpanOp3 ; NOP / OR (HL) / XOR (HL)
$BFB0 PenSpanDone
$BFB2 c PenPixelAddress ; HL = the display-file address of line H, x L (the same code as PresentsPixelAddress $BA6C)
> m1 60 112 calls (PenShaft, PenSpan).
$BFCC b PenNib 3 ; The pen's nib: ten spans (dx, dy, length) from the pen point down-right, $FF
> (0,0,2) (0,1,4) (1,2,5) (1,3,7) (2,4,8) (2,5,10) (3,6,10) (3,7,11) (4,8,11) (4,9,12): a wedge 10 lines high. Read by PenSpans $BE79 (m1, all 31 bytes).
@res graphics/PenNib.bin
$BFEA ; end
$BFEB b PenCap 3 ; The pen's end cap: six spans at (49..54, 54..59), lengths 11 down to 1, $FF
> Read by PenSpans $BE79 after the shaft (m1, all 19 bytes).
@res graphics/PenCap.bin
$BFFD ; end
$BFFE b PenShaftImage 4 ; The shaft: 16 rows of 4 bytes, a 12-pixel bar moving one pixel right a row
> Row r = the bar's 12 pixels starting at pixel r of the 32: PenShaftBlock $BEDC starts at row x AND 7 and takes one row a line. Read by PenShaftCopy4 $BF33 (m1, 60 of 64 bytes: rows 15's last bytes never).
@res graphics/PenShaftImage.bin
$C03E b PenBand ; The pen's state: the band flag (0; 1 / 2 only on the band paths, never run), pen down, the shaft position
> PenBand $C03E: zeroed by SignatureWrite $BD0C, read by SigMove $BD65 (m1 582 reads, always 0).
$C03F PenIsDown ; 0 pen up, 1 pen down (SignatureCommand commands 1 / 2; read by PenStep, m1 1484 reads)
$C040 PenShaftPos ; the shaft's start: x (+0), y (+1) (PenShape, PenShaft)

@module editor ; The table editor: place the balls with a cursor, then play the table
@about The menu's "e" (tests/inputs/m1.txt frame 6260) enters EditorStart $C042: the table with "- editor -", two boxed buttons "play" and "reset" above it, the balls where the last game left them (or the rack), a cross cursor at (128, 115). With the keyboard chosen, the input is switched to the cursor keys for the editor: 5 left, 8 right, 7 up, 6 down, any other key fire (ReadCursorKeys $81C4); CAPS SHIFT + SPACE (BREAK) leaves to the menu.
@about Fire on a ball (within 4 pixels in x and y) picks it up; the same keys then push it through the physics step $8701 (cushions and pockets act on it) and fire puts it down. Fire on "reset" racks the balls; fire on "play" starts a game on the edited table with the game's own aim, power and ball tasks (EditorAimTasks $C518, EditorRollTasks $C528) but no score; when every object ball is down it returns to the menu.
$C042 c EditorStart ; The editor's screen and its cursor loop: move the cursor, fire on a ball or a button
> Entered from the menu ($B293, through a JP after a CALL: the POP HL drops that return). Keeps the input mode $B8BD in EditorSavedMode $C590 and sets mode 3 (cursor keys) when it was 0 (keyboard). Clears the screen (border white, BORDCR $38; attributes $39 on rows 0-5 and 23, $60 on rows 6-22), draws the table ($9243), blanks rows 2-5 columns 18-31, prints "- editor -", "play", "reset" (EditorTexts $C54E) and boxes the two buttons (EditorBox $C1BE: x 148-187 and 204-251, lines 20-35). With no object ball on the table the balls are racked ($9D2C); the balls are drawn ($9520); with the cue ball off the table it is respotted ($9F4E = $FFFF, $93FF). The cursor goes to (128, 115) (EditorCursorHome) and is drawn ($9371).
> The loop (EditorLoop, one HALT a pass): ReadInput $8106; fire with BREAK leaves (EditorExit $C414); fire goes to EditorFire $C20B; a direction moves the cursor by the speed in EditorCursorSpeed $C58D (EditorCursorLeft / Right / Up / Down: x 3..252, y 18..180). The speed: 1 pixel a frame for 15 frames, then one more every 16 frames up to 5; it starts again at 1 when the input changes, is released or the cursor does not move.
> Witness (l1sum.py C042 C116 C196 C20B C414): m1 only: entered at 6262, the loop 6271-6830, 30 passes each of left (6600-6629), down (6640-6669), up (6680-6709), right (6720-6749), fire 110 passes (6420-6769, no ball in reach), BREAK at 6830 (EditorExit).
$C04E EditorKeepMode
$C0DF EditorAnyBall ; is an object ball on the table?
$C0EC EditorDrawBalls
$C102 EditorCursorHome ; the cursor at (128, 115) (also EditorReset's way back)
$C109 EditorCursorShow ; draw the cursor, previous input 0 (EditorFire's way back)
$C111 EditorSpeedReset ; speed 1
$C116 EditorLoop ; one HALT a pass
$C12C EditorKeys
$C151 EditorSpeedUp
$C156 EditorMoveCursor ; erase, move by the speed, draw
$C196 c EditorCursorLeft ; Cursor x - speed, not under 3
> L = x, B = the speed. m1 30 calls (6600-6629).
$C19E EditorLeftEdge
$C1A1 c EditorCursorRight ; Cursor x + speed, not over 252
> m1 30 calls (6720-6749).
$C1A9 EditorRightEdge
$C1AC c EditorCursorUp ; Cursor y - speed, not under 18 (the buttons' line is reachable, the cushion is at 51)
> m1 30 calls (6680-6709).
$C1B5 c EditorCursorDown ; Cursor y + speed, not over 180
> m1 30 calls (6640-6669).
$C1BE c EditorBox ; XOR a rectangle's outline: E, D = left, top; L, H = right, bottom
> Four span calls ($93B3). EditorStart draws the button frames with it: play x 148-187, lines 20-35; reset x 204-251. m1 2 calls (6270).
$C1DD EditorBoxLeft
$C1E9 EditorBoxRight
$C1F1 c EditorInBox ; Z if the cursor is strictly inside the box E, D - L, H (E < x < L, D < y < H)
> The cursor: x $86BA, y $86BB. Play box: x 149-186, y 21-34; reset box: x 205-250, y 21-34. m1 220 calls (two per fire pass), never inside.
$C207 EditorOutsideBox
$C20B c EditorFire ; Fire: "play" or "reset" when the cursor is on them, else pick up the ball under the cursor and move it
> Play box (EditorPlay $C41D), reset box (EditorReset $C532), else the first ball record in order cue, 1-6 (data at $8A88, 14 bytes each) with |x - cursor x| < 5 and |y - cursor y| < 5 on the whole-pixel bytes +1 / +3, on the table or not (a potted ball sits at y 27, x 7 + 24 n: in the cursor's reach). No ball: back to the loop.
> EditorPickBall: EditorPickedBall $C58F = the ball, a beep (BEEPER 1000, 20), the cursor erased. A ball off the table is erased from the potted row, put at (128, 115) and shifted sideways (129, 126, 131, 124 ...) while a ball on the table is closer than 16 pixels in x and in y, then flagged on the table (+13 = $80) and drawn. EditorWaitRelease $C401 waits for fire to go up.
> EditorBallLoop (one HALT a pass): fire puts the ball down (EditorDropBall: a beep, the cursor at the ball, wait for fire up, back to the cursor loop); a direction sets the ball's velocity high bytes (+5 x, +7 y) to +/- the speed in EditorBallSpeed $C58E (the same acceleration as the cursor), fractions and the other velocity zero, and runs one physics step $8701 for it (B holds the speed there, not the ball counter of the physics loop), clears $9F50, keeps the cue ball out of the potted path (+13 bit 5 cleared), moves the cursor with it and redraws it ($8297). When the step took the ball off the table (potted): a beep and back to the cursor loop.
> Witness: m1 110 calls (6420-6769), every one without a ball in reach. e1 (stage 2, the editor recording): 2 calls (frames 700 and 840); at 700 EditorPickBall picks ball 1 of the rack (EditorFindBall twice: the cue ball, then ball 1), EditorHoldBall $C2F4 entered at 702, EditorBallLoop 57 passes, EditorBallMove 20 (frames 720-739), EditorDropBall once; at 840 the play box. The off-table placement EditorPlaceBall $C288 and EditorOverlapLoop ran in no build (known only from the code).
