$C591 b EditorStaleCopy 16 ; 111 bytes equal to $C34C-$C3BA (inside EditorBallMove): a stale copy, never read
> Byte for byte the code from the middle of LD (IX+4),A at $C34A ($C34C) to the opcode of the JP at $C3BA: the copy is cut by the block's end at $C600 before the JP's operand. A leftover of an earlier assembly in which this code sat higher. No build reads it (l1sum.py --reads C591-C5FF).

@module workspace ; The free space and the machine stack below the sprites
@about $C600-$CFFF: 2496 zero bytes no recording reads or writes, then the 64 bytes the machine stack runs in (LD SP,StackTop at GameStart $8000 and BreakRestart $80B0, StackTop = $D000).

$C600 s FreeSpace ; Zeros up to the stack: never read or written in any recording
> 2496 bytes of zeros. tools/l1sum.py --reads C600-CFBF: no read in game, w1, f1, p2, v1, m1, j1. The loader's sum covers them (they are on the tape), nothing else touches them.
$CFC0 s StackSpace ; The machine stack, 64 bytes below $D000
> The stack pointer is set to StackTop ($D000) by GameStart and BreakRestart. The loaded bytes here are zeros; no recording reads a byte before writing it (l1sum.py --reads CFC0-CFFF: none), the lowest byte w1 uses is $CFC4.

@module sprites ; The 56 pre-shifted ball sprites
@about The sprite of ball b (0 the cue ball, 1-6 the object balls) drawn at a screen x is number 7 * (x AND 7) + b, 48 bytes at $D000 + 48 * number (DrawBall $9548): 16 lines of 3 bytes, the left byte first, the 16th line zero, no mask; drawn with XOR so a second draw erases it. Each ball is a 15 x 15 disc with its centre at (7, 7) of the sprite, the object balls with their number cut out. The editable source is src/res/graphics/BallSprites.png, one row a shift, the seven balls left to right.

$D000 b BallSprites 3 ; The 56 ball sprites: 3 bytes x 16 lines, 7 balls x 8 shifts
> Read by DrawBall $9548 (through the XOR drawer): every byte read in game, w1, f1, p2, v1, m1, j1 (l1sum.py --reads D000-DA7F: 2688 of 2688 bytes).
@res graphics/BallSprites.bin

@module penscript ; The pen script: "written by" and the signature, as pen moves
@about SignatureScript $DA80 is the pen's script for SignatureWrite $BD0C: 378 bytes, 312 commands, 557 steps; the pen starts at (16, 128). Byte b: high nibble 0 = command b (0 end, 1 pen up, 2 pen down, 3 dot dx dy with two signed bytes following); otherwise a move of b AND 15 steps by dy = bits 7-6, dx = bits 5-4 (2-bit two's complement: 0, 1, -2, -1), one pixel and one frame a step, plotting (x, y - 1) before each step while the pen is down. The text form for editing is in work/stage2/ann/05-editor-notes.md.
$DA80 b SignatureScript 16 ; The pen script of "written by" (x 16-64) and "James Hutchby" (x 75-184), y 120-146
> Read only by SignatureCommand $BD23 (m1, all 378 bytes, 1830 reads: two whole runs and one cut by a key). Stage 1 called it the logo's data; it is the pen's path. $DBF9 is the end command.
@res text/SignatureScript.bin

@module physicstables ; The physics tables: contact, jaw radius, friction, collision records
@about Four tables of the physics, all read only: ContactTable $DBFA (ball against ball), Table8x8 $DCFA (ball against a jaw point), FrictionTable $DD3A, CollisionRecords $DE3A. Their values fit a construction by d = SQR (dx*dx + dy*dy), see each entry and the notes.
$DBFA b ContactTable 16 ; Ball against ball by |dx| (column) and |dy| (row), whole pixels: $80 overlap, 1-31 the contact ring, 0 apart
> Read by BallContacts $87EF at 16 |dy| + |dx| (|dx|, |dy| < 16): 157 of 256 bytes read (w1, f1, p2, v1, m1; never in game). $80 for every cell with dx*dx + dy*dy <= 212, a record number 1-31 for the 31 cells with 218..250 (d from 14.8 to 15.8: two balls of 15 pixels touching), 0 for 260 and more. The ring numbers run by angle from the x axis: 1 = (15, 0), 16 = (11, 11), 31 = (0, 15); the cell (dx, dy) and (dy, dx) have n and 32 - n.
$DBFA ; |dy| = 0
$DC0A ; |dy| = 1
$DC1A ; |dy| = 2
$DC2A ; |dy| = 3
$DC3A ; |dy| = 4
$DC4A ; |dy| = 5
$DC5A ; |dy| = 6
$DC6A ; |dy| = 7
$DC7A ; |dy| = 8
$DC8A ; |dy| = 9
$DC9A ; |dy| = 10
$DCAA ; |dy| = 11
$DCBA ; |dy| = 12
$DCCA ; |dy| = 13
$DCDA ; |dy| = 14
$DCEA ; |dy| = 15
@res tables/ContactTable.bin
$DCFA b Table8x8 8 ; Ball against a jaw point by |dx| (column) and |dy| (row): $80 overlap, 1 touching, 0 apart
> Read by PointJaw $8CF8 at 8 |dy| + |dx| (twice: $8D1C and the back-off loop $8D41): 16 of 64 bytes read (w1, v1, m1). $80 for dx*dx + dy*dy <= 37, 1 for 40..53 (d 6.3 to 7.3: the ball's radius about 7), 0 for 58 and more. PointJaw also rejects |dx| = |dy| >= 5 before the read, so the cell (5, 5) = 1 is never used.
$DCFA ; |dy| = 0
$DD02 ; |dy| = 1
$DD0A ; |dy| = 2
$DD12 ; |dy| = 3
$DD1A ; |dy| = 4
$DD22 ; |dy| = 5
$DD2A ; |dy| = 6
$DD32 ; |dy| = 7
@res tables/Table8x8.bin
$DD3A b FrictionTable 16 ; Friction steps by the direction: row = |vy| nibble, column = |vx| nibble; high nibble y step, low nibble x step
> Read by FrictionSteps $90F5 at 16 ny + nx: 211 of 256 bytes read in all builds (the unread are mostly small-speed cells; (0, 0), a stopped ball, never). Every entry but (0, 0) = $44 is (round (5 ny / n), round (5 nx / n)) with n = SQR (nx*nx + ny*ny) within 0.5 (row 0: $05, column 0: $50, the diagonal $44): a deceleration of 4.5 to 5.7, about 5, in 1/256 pixel a tick per tick, along the motion.
$DD3A ; ny = 0
$DD4A ; ny = 1
$DD5A ; ny = 2
$DD6A ; ny = 3
$DD7A ; ny = 4
$DD8A ; ny = 5
$DD9A ; ny = 6
$DDAA ; ny = 7
$DDBA ; ny = 8
$DDCA ; ny = 9
$DDDA ; ny = 10
$DDEA ; ny = 11
$DDFA ; ny = 12
$DE0A ; ny = 13
$DE1A ; ny = 14
$DE2A ; ny = 15
@res tables/FrictionTable.bin
$DE3A w CollisionRecords 3 ; 31 records of three words: nx^2, ny^2, |nx ny| for one contact-ring cell (0.8, $0100 = 1.0)
> Record n is ContactTable's ring cell n (dx, dy): w0 = 256 dx*dx / 225, w1 = 256 dy*dy / 225, w2 = 256 dx dy / 225, truncated (29 of 31; records 8 and 24 have 41 for 40.96): the line of centres taken as if the distance were always 15. So w0 + w1 is 1.0 on the axes and 1.075 at 45 degrees (record 16: $89, $89, $89): a diagonal hit gives back up to 7.5 % more than an exact projection. Read by BallContacts $87EF through ContactRecordPtr $89B7 (ScaleVelocity $89C4): 27 of the 31 read (w1, f1, p2, v1, m1); 6, 16, 18, 26 not hit in any recording.
@res tables/CollisionRecords.bin

@module font ; The game's own font: a-z and 0-9
@about The 36 glyphs of 8 x 8 pixels the print routine PrintChar $9E7C takes for the codes $61-$7A (a-z, Font + 8 (code - $61)) and $30-$39 (0-9, Font+208 + 8 (code - $30)); every other code comes from the ROM's CHAR_SET $3D00, $7F from CopyrightGlyph $9F2E. The editable source is src/res/fonts/Font.png, twelve glyphs a row.

$DEF4 b Font 8 ; The font: 26 letters a-z, then 10 digits 0-9, 8 bytes each
> Read by PrintChar $9E7C and the hall of fame's name printers: every byte read in w1 (l1sum.py --reads DEF4-E013: 288 of 288 bytes; game, f1, p2, v1, m1, j1 read the glyphs of their texts).
$DFC4 FontDigits ; the digits 0-9 (Font+208)
@res fonts/Font.bin

@module devleftover ; Code of the development system left in the image: never run, never read
@about $E014-$E0FF is Z80 code that no build executes or reads, followed by zeros to the block's end $E1A7. Its CALLs and data addresses ($44C1, $4200, $506B, $507E, $50A4, $50B2, $5100, $51CC) point into the 48K's display file, so it was assembled for another memory layout. Two pieces: $E014-$E052 counts the zero bits of a bitmap (a free-space count, the shape of a disk's allocation map), and from $E056 an assembler-style text scanner: a case-folding compare, an is-alphanumeric test, a lookup of a name in a table of 8-byte entries (6 characters and a word) and a parser of a parenthesised list of names. With the CALL targets taken as $8FBF bytes below the code ($50A4 = DevIsAlnum $E063, $50B2 = DevLookup $E071) the second piece fits its own calls.
$E014 c DevFreeCount ; Development leftover: count the zero bits of the bitmap at $5100 ($51CC + 35 bytes), store (n + 2) / 4
> Starts mid-routine (BIT 5,(IY+4), POP BC without a PUSH). DE = the number of 0 bits in ($51CC) + 35 bytes from $5100 (SCF / RRA until $FF); then CALL $44C1 and the count + 2, divided by 4, stored at (HL). Never executed or read in any build (l1sum.py --reads E014-E1A7).
$E01B DevFreeCountBody
$E02D DevFreeByteLoop
$E02E DevFreeBitLoop
$E033 DevFreeBitNext
$E053 b DevFragment ; Three bytes ($50, $13, $18) and an endless JR -2 ($FE): no routine start found
$E056 c DevCompareChar ; Development leftover: compare A with (HL), letters case-folded (RES 5 from "A" up)
> Never executed.
$E05C DevCompareFolded
$E05E c DevResultC ; Development leftover: A = C with Z set (LD A,0; OR A; LD A,C)
> Never executed.
$E063 c DevIsAlnum ; Development leftover: carry clear for 0-9, A-Z (the $50A4 of the second piece)
> Never executed.
$E06F DevIsAlnumEnd
$E071 c DevLookup ; Development leftover: find the name at DE in the table at BC (8-byte entries, 0 end); C = index, DE = the word at +6
> Compares the first character, then DevMatchName $E088 for five more (a name ends at CR, $03 or a non-alphanumeric character, padded with spaces). Never executed.
$E076 DevLookupLoop
$E07A DevLookupNext
$E088 c DevMatchName ; Development leftover: the rest of a table name (5 characters); found: DE = the entry's word, Z
> Never executed.
$E08C DevMatchLoop
$E09C DevMatchFound
$E0AA DevMatchDiffer
$E0AF DevMatchEnd
$E0B4 DevMatchFail
$E0B8 b DevParseList 8 ; Development leftover, kept as bytes: skip spaces; "(" name [, name] ")" with each name looked up ($50B2) in the table at DE
> Code, never executed, kept as bytes because its last instruction (XOR A at $E0FF) runs on into the zeros at $E100 and a branch (JR Z at $E0E7) jumps to $E101: the piece is cut off there. Disassembled: $E0B8 INC HL; LD A,(HL); CP $0D; RET Z; CP " "; JR Z,$E0B8; CP "("; JR NZ,$E0FF. $E0C5 PUSH DE; LD B,6; LD DE,$4200; INC HL; CALL $506B (read a name of up to six characters into $4200); DEC HL; POP DE; RET NZ; CP $0D; JR NZ,$E0D7; INC HL. $E0D7 PUSH DE; LD B,D; LD C,E; LD DE,$4200; CALL $50B2 (DevLookup's address in the piece's own layout); JR Z,$E0E4; POP DE; RET. $E0E4 LD A,(HL); CP "="; JR Z,$E101; LD BC,$FFFF; LD (DE),C; INC DE; LD (DE),B (stored through DE as two LD (DE),A); POP DE; LD A,(HL); CP ","; JR Z,$E0C5; CP $0D; JR Z,$E0FF; CP ")"; RET NZ; INC HL. $E0FF XOR A, then the zeros.
$E100 s DevZeros ; Zeros to the end of the loaded block ($E1A7)

